WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
YU SU: I'm super
excited here today

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.710
to talk about a
topic that is really

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.940
close to my heart,
language agents.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.770
I'm very excited about this
topic in the past two years

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:10.580
or so.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.060
It's really fascinating.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.300
And I've devoted most
of my time thinking

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.910
about this, so I have to share
some thoughts here today.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.360
Whether you like
it or not, well,

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.010
since you are in this lecture,
so most likely, you like it,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:28.520
but you probably have heard
about the term agents a lot.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.370
Many people are super
enthusiastic about it.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:34.430
Bill Gates said
that it will bring

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:38.840
about the biggest revolution
since, essentially, GUI.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.050
Then Andrew Ng said that
the agents workflows

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.210
will drive massive AI progress.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:47.360
And then Sam Altman
said that 2025 is

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.240
the year when agents will work.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.820
However, there is
definitely also

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:57.800
a lot of voice from the
other side of the aisle.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:04.580
Many people think that the
current agents are just

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:05.940
thin wrappers around LLM.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.170
So there is nothing
fundamental here.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.400
Well, others think
autoregressive LLMs can never

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.640
truly reason or plan.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.850
And then Auto GPT was one of the
early prototypes of these agent

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.450
systems.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.960
It was very popular, so it's
the fastest growing repository

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:33.180
in GitHub history in
terms of number of stores.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.910
But it didn't take
long for people

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.450
to realize that it was,
at least at the time,

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:42.020
more of a prototype, and that
there was a lot of limitations

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.110
from a production system.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.060
If we take one step back
and think about, hey,

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.410
what is agents?

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.070
It's really not a
new thing, right?

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:59.170
It's one of the very first
topic we cover in AL11 agents,

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:03.250
especially using these
classic schematic illustration

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:04.880
from Russell and Norvig.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.070
And it has been part of
the core pursuits of AI

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:10.430
since the very beginning.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:13.600
So why, all of a sudden, it
became so much more popular

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.940
again?

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:19.990
So let's try to define
this modern agents.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:24.250
Many people hold this view that,
hey, you have a language model.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.110
It has a text in,
text out interface.

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.280
So the things that you
can do is quite limited.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.500
It's still a lot,
but it's limited

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.300
because it's not connected
to external environments.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:39.080
And then once you connect that
to an external environment,

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.410
you can perceive information
from the environment,

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.780
and you can exert impact
on the environment.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.125
Then it becomes an agent.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.100
That seems reasonable,
but at the same time,

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.660
it also seems a bit
oversimplified and incomplete.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:59.560
We often hear things
like a self-reflection

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:05.650
that an agent or LLM looks
at its own reasoning process

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:09.250
and then autonomously
decide to do something else.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.240
Or you can do multi-agent
simulation or lot

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:15.100
of other things that
don't necessarily

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.950
involve external
environment, or at least,

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:23.590
that's not part of the
core defining trait.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:27.700
Fundamentally, I think there
are two main competing views

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.200
in the community.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:34.600
The dominant view, I call
it an LLM-first view.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.830
So first, I have an LLM.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:37.820
It's amazing.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.050
It's very powerful.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.130
It knows a lot of things.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.330
So let's make that
into an agent.

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:47.110
Then if you take this view,
naturally the implications are,

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.810
you think of building a
scaffold on top of an LLM.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.200
And it will be a
lot of prompting.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.330
And it will be heavy
on engineering.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:04:00.360
And I think that's also
where a lot of the sentiments

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:05.910
that the agents are just thin
wrappers around LLMs come from.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.810
I tend to take a different view.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:10.380
I take an agent-based view.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:14.910
So as I said, AI agents have
been the core pursuit of AI

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.360
since the very beginning.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:19.290
And then just now,
they got an upgrade.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:23.440
Now, we integrate one or more
LLMs into these AI agents,

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:26.310
so they gain a new
capability, to use

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:31.050
the language for reasoning
and communication.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:33.510
If you hold this view,
then the implications

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:37.830
are all the same challenges
faced by the previous AI agents

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:38.370
like--

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.030
how to perceive
the environments?

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.190
How to reason about the
environmental state?

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:47.400
How to build world models to
model the state transition

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.970
dynamics in the environment?

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.580
And how to use that
for better planning?

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.890
All of these challenges
still remain,

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:59.340
but we need to re-examine them
through the new lens of LLMs.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.890
And also, we need to
tackle the new challenges

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:03.540
and opportunities.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.920
Now, we can do
synthetic data at scale,

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.510
we can do self-reflection,
and we can also do O1 style

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:10.730
internalized search.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:14.290
OK?

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:17.710
If we agree on that view,
an agent-first view,

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:21.010
then we can look at what's
really fundamentally different

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.280
now once we're
integrating these LLMs.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.660
I think it's really the
capability of using language

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.980
as a vehicle for reasoning
and communication.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.560
The power of using the
language for communication

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.620
is probably more clear.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.540
All of us remember vividly
the viral success of ChatGPT.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:47.010
It was the fastest app to
grow to 100 million users

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:49.102
in human history.

00:05:49.102 --> 00:05:53.100
But we also know that in terms
of the fundamental capabilities,

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.130
ChatGPT was not
significantly better

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:57.320
than the previous
generation, but OpenAI

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:00.350
decided to tune it
to be more chatty

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:02.270
and release it to the public.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:06.110
So I think that really shows
you the power of using language

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:08.900
for communication.

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.350
You get instruction following.

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.060
You get in-context learning.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:17.330
And you can easily
customize the output format.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:20.420
But perhaps, a bit more
unique this time around,

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.370
because using language
for communication,

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:26.850
we have been doing that in
decades in dialogue systems.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.500
But this new capability of
using language for reasoning

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:33.475
is probably a bit more
unique this time around.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:37.820
It used to be the
case that I have

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.860
to do a lot of justification
about why this is unique,

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.440
but now, with all these
new reasoning models,

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.850
I think this has
become much easier.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:49.920
But still consider this example.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.030
Most people would look at
this example and think,

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:57.080
GPT-4 was being stupid
and contradicting itself.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.080
If we start, the answer is
no, but with a lot a bit

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.700
more thinking, then it realizes
that the answer is actually yes.

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:07.490
But the way I see this
example, it really

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:10.470
shows the power of using
language for reasoning.

00:07:10.470 --> 00:07:15.800
So the model can
decide on the fly

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:21.750
to use the adaptive amount of
compute for different problems.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:23.790
So you've got a second chance.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:27.890
Unlike in traditional ML models,
where you pay a fixed amount of

00:07:27.890 --> 00:07:30.300
compute for any decision.

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:33.860
So you only have one shot,
and you have to commit to it.

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:37.020
And especially in the
context of agents,

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:39.990
reasoning is not just for
the sake of reasoning.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:41.790
It's not just for
math or coding.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:44.040
Reasoning is for better acting.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.340
So you can use
reasoning to infer

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.610
about the environmental states,
you can do self-reflection,

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:52.420
you can do dynamic
replanning, but all

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:55.420
for the same purpose for
better acting, better

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:59.440
planning in the environment.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:04.930
Now, we're ready to reconcile
this new generation of language

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:08.320
agents with the previous agents.

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:12.640
All we need to do is to add
a new type of action called

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:17.320
reasoning, by generating
tokens, in contrast with actions

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:20.140
in external environments.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:23.770
Then for that, we also need to
add an internal environment,

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:29.440
just like an inner monologue
where reasoning happens.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:34.179
With that change, now we
can also reconcile things

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:35.600
like self-reflection.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.720
Now, it becomes just a
meta reasoning action,

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:41.240
it's a reasoning over
the reasoning process,

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:45.220
just like the
metacognitive functions.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:46.940
And the reasoning is
for better acting.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:48.730
And at the same time,
due to the power

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:51.880
of these LLMs, the perception
and the external action

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:56.550
spaces are drastically
expanded as well.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:57.050
OK.

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:59.680
Before we proceed,
let me just provide

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.710
a little bit of justification
of the use of reasoning

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:06.440
here, because this is
such an overloaded term.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:11.710
If we don't properly define it,
then people will frown upon it.

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:15.370
So I'm sympathetic
of the position

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:19.660
of you calling all of these as
reasoning, because for people

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:25.390
who are familiar with the
due process, mental model

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:30.430
of human cognition, by
the famous late Daniel

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:32.600
Kahneman in thinking
fast and slow,

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:35.840
you should be familiar with
concepts like perception,

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:39.980
then intuitive inference,
and then symbolic reasoning.

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:42.620
The intuitive inference
is fast, effortless.

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:46.080
And then symbolic reasoning
is slow and effortful.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:52.740
But our LLMs don't have all
of those different cognitive

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.460
substrates for these different
type of cognitive functions.

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:00.820
All it has is mostly
just one mechanism,

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:04.323
which is token generation.

00:10:04.323 --> 00:10:06.240
We will talk about this
later, because we will

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.190
talk about implicit reasoning.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.010
But most of the
reasoning happens

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:14.460
through token generalization,
through explicit language

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.140
production.

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:19.020
So if you look at this
example from GPT-4o,

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.500
when it looks at this
image, its generation

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:27.000
incorporates all of these
different mental processes, what

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:29.760
we would call perception,
what's in there,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.520
and intuitive inference,
what you can immediately

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:36.130
infer from what you see, and
also some symbolic reasoning,

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:38.760
some more complex reasoning.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:42.210
So I think, for this reason,
because LLMs really only

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:46.960
have this one mechanism and
that blends all this together,

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:51.090
so I think it's proper
to call this reasoning.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:55.140
And then one may alternatively
call these thoughts

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.710
to avoid this overloaded
term, but then the risk

00:10:58.710 --> 00:11:02.350
is with the further
anthropomorphize these machines.

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:04.130
So I prefer reasoning
over thoughts.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:11.430
Another thing that I want to
get behind us before we proceed

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:13.320
is the name.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:16.110
There are so many different
names for these agents

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:20.340
right now-- these AI agents,
autonomous agents, LLMs agents,

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:21.490
and so on and so forth.

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:25.110
But I truly think language
agents is probably

00:11:25.110 --> 00:11:31.020
the most discrete characteristic
name for this kind of generation

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:33.900
of agents, because
the language is really

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:37.020
the most salient traits.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:39.160
And what about
multimodal agents?

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:42.420
Well, there is perception
in other modalities,

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:44.870
and they are very
important, but the language

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:47.270
is still doing the heavy
lifting, like the reasoning

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.730
and communication part.

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:51.480
And what about LLMs agents?

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:54.530
That's probably the most
popular name out there.

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:56.840
I think what's
really needed here

00:11:56.840 --> 00:11:59.630
or characteristic of
this generation of agents

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:05.360
is this capability of universal
language understanding

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:07.370
and production,
which turns out to be

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:10.596
extremely important for agents.

00:12:10.596 --> 00:12:15.240
But this capability doesn't
have to come from an LLM.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:18.990
Maybe in a few days, we will go
beyond the LLM, but this need,

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:20.780
this capability will remain.

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:23.910
So LLMs could be
a means to an end.

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.940
In that sense, it's
less as fundamental.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:31.450
So language agents is still
a more appropriate name.

00:12:34.010 --> 00:12:38.870
If we take one step back and
think about the evolution of AI,

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:42.770
I think we're really entering
a new evolutionary stage

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.110
of machine intelligence.

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:48.420
So human intelligence is
truly a marvel made by nature.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:51.620
Somehow, our brain
can take raw inputs

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:55.160
from different sensory
organs and represent them

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:57.590
in a unified neural
representation

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:00.000
to reconstruct the
world around us

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.340
and also to support symbolic
reasoning and decision making.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:07.670
So that's truly fantastic.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:09.690
Then throughout
the history of AI,

00:13:09.690 --> 00:13:13.820
we have made many attempts to
approach human intelligence

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.160
and manifest
machine intelligence

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:18.650
into these AI agents.

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:20.960
But earlier generations
of AI agents

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:24.080
were only able to capture
some limited facets

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.180
of human intelligence,
like symbolic reasoning,

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:31.010
like perception in
single modalities.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:34.820
Only recently was this
multimodal LLMs ends

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:36.890
with language agents
built on top of them.

00:13:36.890 --> 00:13:40.510
For the first time,
we have a model

00:13:40.510 --> 00:13:43.720
that can encode
multisensor inputs

00:13:43.720 --> 00:13:46.810
into a unified neural
representation that

00:13:46.810 --> 00:13:51.430
is also conducive to symbolic
reasoning and communication.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:56.230
So this drastically improves the
expressiveness, the reasoning

00:13:56.230 --> 00:14:00.940
ability, and the
adaptivity of AI agents.

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:04.720
And that's, I think, what makes
this generation of AI agents

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:07.030
so exciting.

00:14:07.030 --> 00:14:09.130
We can do a more
detailed comparison

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:10.940
of these different
generations of agents,

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:13.720
but in the interest of
time, I will just quickly

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:17.230
go through this and just
focus on language agents.

00:14:17.230 --> 00:14:20.170
The expressiveness
is pretty high.

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:23.050
For example, you can compare
with the logical agents, where

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:26.710
the expressiveness is bounded
by the logical language we use.

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:30.520
Here, the agent or the
model can essentially

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:33.730
encode almost
everything, especially

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:35.840
the verbalizable
parts of the world.

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:37.810
But for the
nonverbalizable parts,

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:39.940
like how to recognize
a face, we still

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:45.100
have multimodal encoders
to capture those.

00:14:45.100 --> 00:14:48.760
And then for reasoning, now,
we do language-based reasoning

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:51.350
instead of logical inferences.

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:56.440
So it's fuzzy,
and it's flexible,

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:58.520
and it's semi-explicit.

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:00.598
So it's not entirely implicit.

00:15:00.598 --> 00:15:02.140
You still have this
chain of thoughts

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:03.890
that you can see
what's going on.

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:07.360
And the fuzziness, I want
to emphasize that it's not

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:10.490
necessarily a bad thing,
because the world around us,

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:14.080
it has a lot of fuzziness in it.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:18.490
If we resort to something
strictly very sound and very

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:23.260
rigid, then it comes as
a cost of sacrificing

00:15:23.260 --> 00:15:25.900
the expressiveness a lot.

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:30.170
And then the adaptivity, which
is a hallmark of intelligence,

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:32.530
is also very high
in language agents,

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:35.750
because these are strong
prior captured by an LLM,

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:38.345
and the language used in
general is very flexible.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.920
So before we proceed,
let me share with you

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:46.720
my own conceptual framework
for language agents.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:49.950
This is what I have been using
to guide my own research agenda

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:53.430
in the past two years or so.

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:58.090
The most important things are
what I call core competencies.

00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:01.210
And I try to arrange them
in a loose hierarchy.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:04.050
So each box here,
you can roughly

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:07.630
find the corresponding cognitive
function in the human brain.

00:16:07.630 --> 00:16:11.610
And then the bottom ones are
the more fundamental ones,

00:16:11.610 --> 00:16:14.200
like perception,
memory, and embodiment.

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:17.440
And the upper ones built
on top of the bottom ones,

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:20.460
like planning built on top
of reasoning and word models,

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:23.060
and reasoning built on top
of perception and memory,

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:24.840
and so on, so forth.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:27.300
We also have many
cross-cutting issues,

00:16:27.300 --> 00:16:30.540
like safety, evaluation,
synthetic data

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:34.690
is both a challenge and an
opportunity, and efficiency,

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:36.610
and many new applications.

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:39.330
So with this
framework, now, you can

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:41.910
look at any new
paper about agents

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:46.830
and try to map its main
claims and main contributions

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:48.820
into this framework.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:54.310
So that's the introduction.

00:16:54.310 --> 00:16:57.640
And for the rest of
the talk, we will

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:02.100
try to cover three main
aspects of language agents.

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:05.099
How we model long-term
memory, for which,

00:17:05.099 --> 00:17:08.130
I will use our recent
work, HippoRAG.

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:12.040
Then how do these language
models fully reason?

00:17:12.040 --> 00:17:15.040
And I will use our work
on Grokked Transformer.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:18.000
And then finally, I will talk
about planning, especially

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.980
word-based planning
using a word model.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:21.480
OK.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.705
So let's start with memory.

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:28.740
We'll talk about HippoRAG.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.630
The main content will
be from HippoRAG,

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:34.130
but we'll talk about a lot
of other things as well.

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:37.190
It's a neurobiologically
inspired long-term memory

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:39.150
mechanism for large
language models.

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:43.190
It's led by my student,
Bernal, and in collaboration

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:44.320
with Michi from Stanford.

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:51.650
So if we look at how
humans or really,

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:56.310
most other animals that have a
nervous system, how they learn,

00:17:56.310 --> 00:18:02.630
it's really fascinating that
we are all this 24/7, nonstop,

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:05.070
lifelong learners.

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:10.250
And all we learn is
stored in our memory.

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:15.200
So Eric Kandel, the
Nobel Prize winner

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:19.530
who got the prize
for his contribution

00:18:19.530 --> 00:18:21.560
in the study of
memory, especially

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:24.090
the neurobiological
foundation of memory,

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:27.510
once said that
memory is everything.

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:30.320
Without it, we are
nothing, which, I think,

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:35.990
is very profound, because
really, anything we learn

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:41.630
has to be encoded in our memory
and through a process called

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:44.510
synaptic plasticity.

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:48.855
So basically, we can change
our synapses to memorize things

00:18:48.855 --> 00:18:50.855
and to capture all the
things we're experiencing

00:18:50.855 --> 00:18:52.550
and we're learning.

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:57.150
And there are different ways
of changing the synapses.

00:18:57.150 --> 00:19:00.950
For example, you can change
the strength of the synapses

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:08.390
by either adding more
receptors here or releasing

00:19:08.390 --> 00:19:11.300
more neurotransmitters.

00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:16.670
Or you can do structural
changes to the synapses,

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:22.170
by growing new synapses for
the same neuron, for example.

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:27.170
So this is used, more often,
in forming long-term memory.

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:30.320
So we're really 24/7 learners.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:34.690
Even when we are
sleeping, we are replaying

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:36.740
what happens during the day.

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:41.270
That's how this long-term
memory gets consolidated.

00:19:41.270 --> 00:19:47.230
Ideally, we want the same kind
of learning capacity in machines

00:19:47.230 --> 00:19:50.650
or especially, in agents as
well, because these agents, they

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:53.570
are supposed to
explore the world,

00:19:53.570 --> 00:19:56.770
do things, and then
accumulate a lot of learning

00:19:56.770 --> 00:20:00.790
from that, and
self-improve, in some sense.

00:20:00.790 --> 00:20:07.450
However, that's very hard
for the current technologies.

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:12.550
For these neural networks, in
general, and this gigantic LLMs,

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:18.160
in particular, they
have the notorious issue

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:22.180
of catastrophic forgetting.

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:27.290
Because of these highly
distributional representation

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:30.110
in these models, when you
are learning new things,

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:33.530
it often has unintended
side effects,

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:37.540
like some other
things unexpectedly

00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:41.630
got changed during that
new learning process.

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:47.680
And to illustrate this,
in the LLM context,

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:50.200
we can consider what
people have been

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:55.960
doing, trying to edit an
LLM to inject or alter

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:57.470
a specific effect.

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:00.920
So that's called
knowledge model editing.

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:05.980
And a hot topic in
LLMs model editing

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:09.010
is the so-called ripple effect.

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:12.370
So if you want to
change just this one

00:21:12.370 --> 00:21:17.450
fact in a counterfactual
change, you want to say,

00:21:17.450 --> 00:21:25.220
hey, Leonardo is a citizen of
Syria instead of United States.

00:21:25.220 --> 00:21:29.500
And you have some
expected ripple effect,

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:32.670
because now, the
citizenship has changed,

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:37.050
so he should speak Arabic
instead of English.

00:21:37.050 --> 00:21:41.980
But if you actually look at what
has changed after these edits,

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:45.970
you will see that a lot of other
unexpected things have changed,

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:51.010
or the things that should have
changed but didn't change.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:55.270
So if you look at the negation
of these original statements,

00:21:55.270 --> 00:22:00.370
you will see that it will still
predict Syria, which is wrong.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:03.760
You want it to be United
States because of the negation.

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:11.200
And then the language
here also becomes Syria,

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.050
but you want it to be Arabic,
and so on and so forth.

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:19.170
So all of these just tell you
that because of this highly

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:22.830
distributional representation
of these artificial neural

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:26.085
networks, that makes
continual learning very hard.

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:32.100
The human brain
and animals somehow

00:22:32.100 --> 00:22:34.290
figure out a way to do
that, but we don't quite

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:36.850
understand how that happens yet.

00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:40.710
So we cannot replicate
that in machines.

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:43.740
But this kind of
continual learning

00:22:43.740 --> 00:22:47.620
is highly desired for
agents and for LLMs.

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:50.520
So what can we do?

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:54.540
The good news is
that for LLMs, it's

00:22:54.540 --> 00:22:58.230
possible to use an
alternative form of memory

00:22:58.230 --> 00:23:01.380
called nonparametric memory.

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:04.860
So instead of directly
changing the parameters

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:08.230
of a model with the
new experiences,

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:10.230
we can just hold
the new experience

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:14.080
external to the model,
so it's nonparametric,

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:18.905
and then just retrieve them
using some mechanism as we go.

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:27.390
Of course, this is called a RAG,
Retrieval Augmented Generation.

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:33.440
But for that to work there is a
prerequisite condition, that is,

00:23:33.440 --> 00:23:38.640
when you retrieve some external
information, and you say,

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:42.630
hey, this is your memory,
so you should take it

00:23:42.630 --> 00:23:45.030
or your decision making
should be based on it.

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:49.970
Then it's based on the condition
that these LLMs actually

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:55.971
will be receptive to such
external information.

00:23:55.971 --> 00:24:00.590
So we did this study
called adaptive comedian

00:24:00.590 --> 00:24:04.160
or stubborn thoughts,
where we specifically

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:05.640
study this behavior.

00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:09.600
When you have this
external evidence from RAG,

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:11.930
and then when that
directly conflict

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:15.780
within the LLM's parametric
memory, what would happen?

00:24:15.780 --> 00:24:21.110
Will they resist that or will
they be receptive to that?

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:24.270
Perhaps, not too
surprisingly at this point,

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:26.550
but was very
surprising at the time

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:29.810
is that these LLMs
turned out to be highly

00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:32.990
receptive to external
evidence, even

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:36.380
when that conflict with
their parametric memory.

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:38.420
So for the three
examples here, you

00:24:38.420 --> 00:24:40.470
have a question and the
ground truth answer.

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:44.730
In this case, the parametric
memory of the LLM is correct.

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:49.140
But if you give it a coherent
counter memory like this,

00:24:49.140 --> 00:24:53.030
then the LLM will happily accept
this and change its answer.

00:24:53.030 --> 00:24:56.730
And in this case, the
parametric memory was wrong,

00:24:56.730 --> 00:25:00.140
and you can correct that by
giving it the correct quantum

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:00.870
memory.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:05.300
So that paves the way
for nonparametric memory

00:25:05.300 --> 00:25:08.990
for LLMs because of
their being highly

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:12.380
receptive to this
external evidence.

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:15.500
Of course, this has other
implications like safety.

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:19.910
Maybe it means that these
LLMs could be highly callable,

00:25:19.910 --> 00:25:25.030
but that's not the focus today.

00:25:25.030 --> 00:25:26.230
OK.

00:25:26.230 --> 00:25:29.950
Now, let's talk about how to
really make long-term memory

00:25:29.950 --> 00:25:32.770
work for LLMs.

00:25:32.770 --> 00:25:37.670
Most people know that RAG is
the de facto solution today.

00:25:37.670 --> 00:25:42.910
You are given something that is,
for example, the LLMs doesn't

00:25:42.910 --> 00:25:45.980
know or is beyond its
knowledge cutoff date.

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:48.100
Then it will retrieve
from the internet

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:50.020
and then use the
retrieved information

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:56.260
as a long-term memory
to answer the question.

00:25:56.260 --> 00:26:00.170
But if you think
about how RAG works,

00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:03.260
you embed this
evidence into vectors,

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:06.640
then you do vector-based
similarity to retrieve them,

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:12.310
that seems far simpler,
far less sophisticated

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:17.290
than the human memory system,
where we can recognize patterns

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:20.840
in massive data,
raw experiences.

00:26:20.840 --> 00:26:24.670
We can create a lot of
associations across them.

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:29.630
And we can dynamically retrieve
them for the current context,

00:26:29.630 --> 00:26:30.680
and so on and so forth.

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:34.750
So to illustrate some
of the limitations

00:26:34.750 --> 00:26:37.600
of the current
embedding-based RAG system,

00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:40.300
let's consider this example.

00:26:40.300 --> 00:26:43.780
Let's say, you have a query--

00:26:43.780 --> 00:26:46.450
which Stanford professor
works on the neuroscience

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:48.070
of Alzheimer's.

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:50.240
So you have two
salient concepts--

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:52.480
Stanford and Alzheimer's.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:55.600
Then let's assume just
hypothetically, you

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:58.030
have a bunch of passages.

00:26:58.030 --> 00:27:02.450
Each passage only contains
parts of the information.

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:06.970
So you don't happen to have
a passage that tells you,

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:10.900
this person is both a
Stanford professor and works

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:12.080
on Alzheimer's.

00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:15.920
Instead, what you have is
these separate passages.

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:19.510
OK, this passage says this
person works at Stanford.

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:23.400
This passage says that this
person works on Alzheimer's.

00:27:23.400 --> 00:27:27.210
And then you will see,
there is one person who

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:31.410
works at Stanford and
on Alzheimer's, but it's

00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:33.100
in separate documents.

00:27:33.100 --> 00:27:37.600
You don't happen to have one
document that tells you all.

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:42.510
So what would happen if you
just use embedding based on RAG?

00:27:42.510 --> 00:27:46.590
Now, you embed each of these
passages into a vector.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:48.850
You embed your
query into a vector.

00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:52.000
You do compare these
similarities one by one.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.900
Then you will find,
all of these passages

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:58.350
are equally likely
because each of them

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:03.670
captures precisely 50%
of the information.

00:28:03.670 --> 00:28:09.270
And as a result, the
model has to manually go

00:28:09.270 --> 00:28:12.660
through all of these
passages to figure out which

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:15.300
ones are the correct ones.

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:18.810
And you can imagine there,
could be thousands of professors

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:22.290
working at Stanford and
even more people working

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:23.490
on Alzheimer's.

00:28:23.490 --> 00:28:27.750
So it's a one to many,
many to one relationship.

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:31.080
But our human memory
doesn't work that way.

00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:36.750
Somehow, if we have come
across like this person works

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:39.820
on Alzheimer's, this person
also works at Stanford,

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:44.160
then we will build some sort of
association among them such that

00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:48.090
when this query comes, we can
very easily and quickly find

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:51.630
that, hey, this person is
connected with both Stanford

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:52.840
and Alzheimer's.

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:54.370
So it's the answer.

00:28:54.370 --> 00:28:56.880
We don't have to
go through, recall

00:28:56.880 --> 00:29:03.180
all of this other information
to arrive at that conclusion.

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:05.880
So in that sense,
embedding based in RAG

00:29:05.880 --> 00:29:08.270
works very differently
from human memory.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:17.990
Now, to study how exactly how
the human memory works in these

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:22.100
regards, so we turn to this
well-established theory

00:29:22.100 --> 00:29:26.780
of human long-term memory called
the hippocampal indexing theory.

00:29:26.780 --> 00:29:28.370
It basically goes like this.

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:33.230
I'll summarize it in an
overly simplified way.

00:29:33.230 --> 00:29:38.070
It says that your raw memory
is stored on your neocortex,

00:29:38.070 --> 00:29:44.880
particularly, where the
memory was first generated.

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:49.370
So for an episodic memory,
the auditory part of it

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:52.080
will be stored in
the auditory cortex.

00:29:52.080 --> 00:29:55.350
The visual part of it will be
stored in the visual cortex,

00:29:55.350 --> 00:29:56.340
and so on and so forth.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.220
That's why when you are
trying to recall something,

00:29:59.220 --> 00:30:01.290
you were like
reliving the moments,

00:30:01.290 --> 00:30:05.840
because it's just really
triggering the same neurons

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:09.540
as if you were perceiving them.

00:30:09.540 --> 00:30:14.360
But importantly, you also
have a structured index

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:17.930
stored in the
hippocampus that creates

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:22.160
a much closer,
essentially, a shortcut

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:28.556
that associates all these
disparate memory units together.

00:30:28.556 --> 00:30:33.980
And that's what we are
trying to mimic here.

00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:36.980
This kind of separation
and structure index

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:41.120
gives you two important
faculties of human memory.

00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:43.710
It allows you to do
pattern separation.

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:48.680
So you can differentiate
memories in a very fine-grained

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:52.340
way, certainly, beyond
just vectors, at least,

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:53.760
at the concept level.

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:58.580
If you think about your
episodic memory, this second

00:30:58.580 --> 00:31:01.740
and the second before, they
are very similar to each other.

00:31:01.740 --> 00:31:04.590
How can you differentiate
the differences?

00:31:04.590 --> 00:31:08.930
That requires very fine-grained
separation of patterns.

00:31:08.930 --> 00:31:11.810
But more importantly, which
is more relevant here,

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:13.980
is pattern completion.

00:31:13.980 --> 00:31:19.380
We can recover complete memories
using just partial cues.

00:31:19.380 --> 00:31:22.940
Like the previous example,
the partial cues are Stanford

00:31:22.940 --> 00:31:24.500
and Alzheimer's.

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:27.980
And then you can quickly
recall this whole fact

00:31:27.980 --> 00:31:30.990
that this person is
associated with both of them.

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:34.400
And that's due to
this structured index

00:31:34.400 --> 00:31:37.790
in the hippocampus.

00:31:37.790 --> 00:31:43.460
So that's what we're trying
to mimic in HippoRAG.

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:48.230
We're trying to build a similar
structured index for RAG systems

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:51.290
to enjoy some of
the same benefits

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:53.065
of the human long-term
memory system.

00:31:56.150 --> 00:31:59.460
I won't get into
too much details,

00:31:59.460 --> 00:32:06.600
so just at a high level, in the
hippocampal indexing theory,

00:32:06.600 --> 00:32:08.550
there are three main parts--

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.400
there is the neocortex,
and the hippocampus,

00:32:11.400 --> 00:32:14.930
and then there is the parahippo
regions that connects the two.

00:32:14.930 --> 00:32:20.030
So the neocortex is more
about pattern separation.

00:32:20.030 --> 00:32:22.880
So it will process
the raw experience,

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:26.300
extract patterns out
of these concepts,

00:32:26.300 --> 00:32:27.380
and so on and so forth.

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:31.250
And then the hippocampus is
more like the structure index,

00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:33.980
so it's the indexing
auto-association.

00:32:33.980 --> 00:32:35.980
And then the
hippocampal regions is

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:37.630
more like the
working memory that

00:32:37.630 --> 00:32:42.940
connects the two, then do
some more iterative thinking

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:45.520
around there.

00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:51.320
So to mimic this process,
we have two spaces--

00:32:51.320 --> 00:32:54.670
we have the offline indexing
phase and the online query

00:32:54.670 --> 00:32:55.370
phase.

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:59.410
For the offline indexing, let's
say, you have these passages

00:32:59.410 --> 00:33:00.470
as inputs.

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:04.720
We use an LLM to serve
as a neocortex that

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:08.980
will do open information
extraction to extract

00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:10.660
these triplets--

00:33:10.660 --> 00:33:14.230
the concepts, noun phrases,
and their relationships,

00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:16.540
like the verb phrases.

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:21.070
So extract these
triplets, then we

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:23.560
do try to build a
knowledge graph.

00:33:23.560 --> 00:33:26.750
Particularly, this is a
schema that's knowledge graph.

00:33:26.750 --> 00:33:29.800
So we don't have an ontology,
or a predefined schema,

00:33:29.800 --> 00:33:31.100
or anything like that.

00:33:31.100 --> 00:33:36.820
Everything is extracted by an
LLM from the raw experiences.

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:41.200
So we built a knowledge
graph by consolidating

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:45.010
all these newly extracted
concepts and phrases

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:46.790
as nodes and edges.

00:33:46.790 --> 00:33:49.910
That's the offline
indexing phase.

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:56.350
And this becomes our
artificial hippocampal index.

00:33:56.350 --> 00:34:01.330
Then we use a dense
retrieval here as an encoder

00:34:01.330 --> 00:34:03.940
to consolidate
things, to identify

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:08.170
which concepts are similar
to each other or synonymous

00:34:08.170 --> 00:34:10.170
to each other.

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:14.710
Then in the online retrieval
phase, when a query comes in,

00:34:14.710 --> 00:34:18.100
like the Stanford
assignment example,

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:20.290
then we'll identify the concept.

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:22.110
We use named entity recognition.

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:24.840
So now, we'll get the
Stanford and Alzheimer's.

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:29.820
We still use a dense
retrieval to find

00:34:29.820 --> 00:34:35.190
the similar nodes in my index.

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:41.250
And then those nodes become the
seed nodes for the graph search

00:34:41.250 --> 00:34:42.159
process.

00:34:42.159 --> 00:34:47.498
So we will use these as seeds to
do a search process on the graph

00:34:47.498 --> 00:34:50.760
to find the most related things.

00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:55.440
And this particular graph search
algorithm we're using here

00:34:55.440 --> 00:34:58.790
is Personalized PageRank.

00:34:58.790 --> 00:34:59.290
OK.

00:34:59.290 --> 00:35:01.950
So for people who
don't quite remember

00:35:01.950 --> 00:35:06.760
what a Personalized PageRank
works, I'll do a quick recap.

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:10.500
So it's a random walk
process on the graph, where

00:35:10.500 --> 00:35:12.310
you start with some seed nodes.

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:15.520
So those start
with probability 1.

00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:18.780
Then you use a random walk
starting from the seed

00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:21.750
nodes to disperse
the probability mass

00:35:21.750 --> 00:35:24.220
to their neighboring nodes.

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:27.720
So the nodes that are
closer to these seed

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:31.110
nodes or especially, those
that are in intersection

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:33.660
of multiple seed
nodes will naturally

00:35:33.660 --> 00:35:35.760
end up with a higher weights.

00:35:35.760 --> 00:35:38.770
Then in this case,
Professor Thomas,

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:41.710
who is connected to both
Stanford and Alzheimer's

00:35:41.710 --> 00:35:45.490
will naturally stand out and
getting the highest weights.

00:35:45.490 --> 00:35:48.090
Then we can use these
weights over concept

00:35:48.090 --> 00:35:51.340
to reweight the
original passages,

00:35:51.340 --> 00:35:56.700
then to retrieve the most
highly weighted passage.

00:35:56.700 --> 00:36:00.190
So that's how HippoRAG
works in a nutshell.

00:36:00.190 --> 00:36:03.490
Of course, there are a lot of
details that we're not covering,

00:36:03.490 --> 00:36:08.000
but this is the main gist.

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:12.380
And it turns out,
some simple strategy

00:36:12.380 --> 00:36:16.730
like this, biologically
plausible strategy,

00:36:16.730 --> 00:36:20.460
works also extremely
well in practice.

00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:21.920
It's not enough.

00:36:21.920 --> 00:36:25.040
It is, of course, a neat
idea, like, hey, this

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:27.020
is biologically inspired.

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:28.620
How good is that?

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:31.040
But I think it's
equally important for it

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:36.440
to actually work, to work very
well and better than existing

00:36:36.440 --> 00:36:38.550
solutions in practice.

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:41.120
And that's what we
showed with HippoRAG.

00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:45.020
So we'll compare HippoRAG with
the state-of-the-art dense

00:36:45.020 --> 00:36:53.240
retrievers at the time and show
that three standard multi-hop QA

00:36:53.240 --> 00:36:58.410
datasets, it performs much
better by a large margin.

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:01.640
And also, you can
compare HippoRAG

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:06.600
with this iterative
retrieval methods like IRCoT.

00:37:06.600 --> 00:37:11.340
And it also works better
than this iterative method,

00:37:11.340 --> 00:37:15.270
and also because HippoRAG,
the nature of it, really,

00:37:15.270 --> 00:37:18.010
this structured index
and this graph search,

00:37:18.010 --> 00:37:22.630
so it's highly complementary
to all these existing methods.

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:27.270
You can easily integrate it
with other methods like IRCoT.

00:37:27.270 --> 00:37:30.635
And then you get a
big boost as well.

00:37:33.540 --> 00:37:34.280
OK.

00:37:34.280 --> 00:37:39.240
And to better understand where
does this power of HippoRAG

00:37:39.240 --> 00:37:42.690
comes from, we can consider
a type of questions,

00:37:42.690 --> 00:37:46.470
what we call
path-finding questions,

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:50.640
like the running example
we have been working with.

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:53.910
So if you think about,
in this information

00:37:53.910 --> 00:37:57.360
space, like this huge graph
of everything connected

00:37:57.360 --> 00:37:59.490
to everything,
what's the solution

00:37:59.490 --> 00:38:04.390
path or structure for
this question looks like?

00:38:04.390 --> 00:38:11.500
You will see, it's not just a
one to one to one kind of task,

00:38:11.500 --> 00:38:14.560
you first start with a
one to many relation.

00:38:14.560 --> 00:38:17.960
Like Stanford, there are many
professors working at Stanford.

00:38:17.960 --> 00:38:22.140
Then among those is our
answer, Professor Thomas.

00:38:24.880 --> 00:38:27.910
And then from the
people to researchers

00:38:27.910 --> 00:38:32.150
to Alzheimer's, that's also
a many to one relationship.

00:38:32.150 --> 00:38:34.960
So there are many people
working on Alzheimer's.

00:38:34.960 --> 00:38:38.860
If you don't have prior
knowledge about the answer

00:38:38.860 --> 00:38:41.020
you're looking for,
then naturally, you'd

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:45.880
have to search through all
these hundreds of thousands

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:49.750
of candidates for you to
find Professor Thomas.

00:38:49.750 --> 00:38:53.920
And HippoRAG, by explicitly
extracting, and building

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:57.560
these associations
from the raw inputs,

00:38:57.560 --> 00:39:01.810
that allows you to create
these shortcuts to quickly find

00:39:01.810 --> 00:39:02.870
the true answer.

00:39:02.870 --> 00:39:05.750
While if you use
ColBERT or IRCoT,

00:39:05.750 --> 00:39:08.406
then they won't be able
to find it efficiently.

00:39:11.460 --> 00:39:11.960
OK.

00:39:11.960 --> 00:39:14.290
So that was HippoRAG.

00:39:14.290 --> 00:39:18.710
That was published in NeurIPS,
which is just two months ago,

00:39:18.710 --> 00:39:23.320
but in today's pace,
it feels like ages ago.

00:39:23.320 --> 00:39:30.160
So I'm very excited to
share that HippoRAG v2 is

00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:31.460
coming very soon.

00:39:31.460 --> 00:39:37.480
And so a problem with HippoRAG,
all these recent structured

00:39:37.480 --> 00:39:41.320
augmented RAG methods, like
GraphRAG, LightRAG, RAPTOR,

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:43.210
and so on and so forth.

00:39:43.210 --> 00:39:51.760
If you compare favorably
to this simpler baselines

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:54.730
like these small
dense retrievals

00:39:54.730 --> 00:39:57.770
like ColBERT v2 or Contriever.

00:39:57.770 --> 00:40:01.770
But recently, there have been
many large embedding models,

00:40:01.770 --> 00:40:04.330
like Grit, like NV-Embed.

00:40:04.330 --> 00:40:08.160
And if you actually compare
these structured augmented RAG

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:11.920
methods with them, you will
see that they are much worse,

00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:14.250
including HippoRAG.

00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:20.400
They are working compare OK
on these multi-HOP QA tasks

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:24.030
because they will
mostly be designed

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:25.300
for these kind of tasks.

00:40:25.300 --> 00:40:28.080
But if you just look
at other scenarios,

00:40:28.080 --> 00:40:31.890
like just some very simple QA
or some discourse understanding,

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:34.750
and so on and so forth,
they don't work that well.

00:40:34.750 --> 00:40:37.830
So as a result, it's very
hard for these methods

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:40.560
to become just a
drop in replacement

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:44.130
of embedding-based methods.

00:40:44.130 --> 00:40:50.490
Then in HippoRAG v2, we did
a bunch of clever upgrades

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:52.510
to HippoRAG v1.

00:40:52.510 --> 00:40:56.070
And the result is
that now, v2 is

00:40:56.070 --> 00:40:59.730
comparable or better
than the best logic

00:40:59.730 --> 00:41:02.520
embedding models
across the board.

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:06.600
So then it's much more
plausible to just use

00:41:06.600 --> 00:41:12.450
HippoRAG v2 as a drop in
replacement for your RAG system.

00:41:12.450 --> 00:41:16.440
We hope to release
this very soon.

00:41:16.440 --> 00:41:20.550
So for the memory
part, the takeaways,

00:41:20.550 --> 00:41:24.840
so memory is really
central to human learning.

00:41:24.840 --> 00:41:28.890
And long-term memory through
parametric continual learning

00:41:28.890 --> 00:41:31.150
for LLMs is very hard.

00:41:31.150 --> 00:41:34.530
But fortunately,
non-parametric memory like RAG

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:36.720
could be a promising solution.

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:40.350
And the recent trend in RAG
is to add more structures

00:41:40.350 --> 00:41:42.720
to embeddings, like
HippoRAG or GraphRAG,

00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:47.140
to enhance the sense-making
capability of these models,

00:41:47.140 --> 00:41:49.470
like the ability to
integrate larger, more

00:41:49.470 --> 00:41:54.210
complex, and uncertain
contexts, and the associativity

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:56.450
of these memory,
like the capacity

00:41:56.450 --> 00:42:00.440
to draw multi-HOP connections
between disparate pieces

00:42:00.440 --> 00:42:01.560
of information.

00:42:04.370 --> 00:42:04.920
OK?

00:42:04.920 --> 00:42:07.050
So that's memory.

00:42:07.050 --> 00:42:09.740
And I think we're
still quite far away

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:13.770
from developing a very
sophisticated memory system,

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:17.780
but we are getting there.

00:42:17.780 --> 00:42:19.760
But there are still
many gaps like how

00:42:19.760 --> 00:42:23.900
to handle like episodic memory,
the spatial temporal aspects

00:42:23.900 --> 00:42:26.960
of things, which is
central to human memory.

00:42:26.960 --> 00:42:29.330
We don't have a
good solution yet.

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:29.830
OK.

00:42:29.830 --> 00:42:33.830
So memory is the most
fundamental aspects

00:42:33.830 --> 00:42:35.180
in my opinion.

00:42:35.180 --> 00:42:41.420
Then let's get to another
very fundamental aspect, which

00:42:41.420 --> 00:42:46.670
can be built on top of
memory, which is reasoning.

00:42:46.670 --> 00:42:48.950
And our discussion today
will be mainly based

00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:51.230
on this paper,
Grokking of Implicit

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:53.780
Relations in Transformers.

00:42:53.780 --> 00:42:57.720
It's led by the student
from our group, Boshi Wang.

00:42:57.720 --> 00:43:02.556
And it's published
in NeurIPS last year.

00:43:02.556 --> 00:43:03.380
All right.

00:43:03.380 --> 00:43:08.600
So we will be talking
about implicit reasoning.

00:43:08.600 --> 00:43:12.750
In implicit reasoning, we
don't do chain of thought.

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:16.590
So there is no verbalized
chain of thoughts explicitly.

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:21.560
We ask the LLM to directly
predict the answer.

00:43:21.560 --> 00:43:25.190
In this example of
compositional reasoning,

00:43:25.190 --> 00:43:30.530
let's say, the language model
has memorized or know these two

00:43:30.530 --> 00:43:34.500
atomic facts that
Barack's wife is Michelle,

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:37.620
and Michelle was born in 1964.

00:43:37.620 --> 00:43:42.350
Then the model will be given
these inputs, like Barack,

00:43:42.350 --> 00:43:46.340
wife, born in, and is
asked to directly predict

00:43:46.340 --> 00:43:52.940
the answer, 1964, in this
compositional reasoning fashion.

00:43:52.940 --> 00:43:54.980
So if you do chain of
thoughts, of course,

00:43:54.980 --> 00:43:58.520
you can try to recall, hey,
Barack's wife is Michelle.

00:43:58.520 --> 00:43:59.660
Michelle was born in this.

00:43:59.660 --> 00:44:01.250
So therefore, the
answer is this.

00:44:01.250 --> 00:44:05.530
But we want to push the
model to implicitly just

00:44:05.530 --> 00:44:08.170
using its parameters in
a single forward pass

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:10.630
to do this reasoning.

00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:12.190
Why?

00:44:12.190 --> 00:44:17.060
CoT, especially this long CoT
is all the rage right now,

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:19.250
like in R1 or o1.

00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:24.460
And why does implicit
reasoning matter?

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:28.720
This is also, in
the beginning, why

00:44:28.720 --> 00:44:30.670
the reasoning
mechanism of these LLMs

00:44:30.670 --> 00:44:35.680
is mostly just token generation,
but implicit reasoning

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:39.550
is still part of the
reasoning repertoire.

00:44:39.550 --> 00:44:42.460
So why does implicit
reasoning matter?

00:44:42.460 --> 00:44:46.810
First of all, this
is the default mode

00:44:46.810 --> 00:44:50.290
of training or pre-training.

00:44:50.290 --> 00:44:53.530
Because during training
time, when the model

00:44:53.530 --> 00:44:57.980
is asked to predict the next
token with cross-entropy loss,

00:44:57.980 --> 00:45:01.250
there is no CoT, at
least, not for now.

00:45:01.250 --> 00:45:06.920
So in training time, model
has to compress the data.

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:13.120
It has to do implicit reasoning
in order to reasonably predict

00:45:13.120 --> 00:45:14.450
the next token.

00:45:14.450 --> 00:45:18.520
So no matter what, I think
it's important to understand

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:23.650
the implicit reasoning
capability of language models.

00:45:23.650 --> 00:45:25.960
Also, implicit
reasoning fundamentally

00:45:25.960 --> 00:45:29.050
determines how well
these models acquire

00:45:29.050 --> 00:45:31.270
structured representations
of the facts

00:45:31.270 --> 00:45:33.850
and the rules from
their training data.

00:45:33.850 --> 00:45:38.560
And finally, there is
a lot of speculation

00:45:38.560 --> 00:45:45.760
about how these o1 or R1
style long CoT emerge from RL.

00:45:45.760 --> 00:45:49.310
I think, one plausible
hypothesis, as it is in my mind,

00:45:49.310 --> 00:45:52.110
is like this.

00:45:52.110 --> 00:45:54.510
You start with a
capable base model.

00:45:54.510 --> 00:45:59.100
If the base model is not capable
enough, then RL won't do much.

00:45:59.100 --> 00:46:02.460
So you start with the capable
base model, like DeepSeek v3.

00:46:02.460 --> 00:46:04.740
And it probably
has already learned

00:46:04.740 --> 00:46:08.460
all of these basic
constructs or strategies

00:46:08.460 --> 00:46:11.620
for reasoning, so
like self-reflection,

00:46:11.620 --> 00:46:15.630
like analogical reasoning,
and so on and so forth,

00:46:15.630 --> 00:46:18.100
maybe in implicit
reasoning fashion,

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:22.320
just some kind of reasoning
circuits in the parameters.

00:46:22.320 --> 00:46:26.310
Then reinforcement learning
was these verifiable rewards,

00:46:26.310 --> 00:46:30.090
is just to incentivize
the model to learn

00:46:30.090 --> 00:46:32.890
to use the right
combination of strategies.

00:46:32.890 --> 00:46:37.170
But it's not learning new
reasoning strategies through RL.

00:46:37.170 --> 00:46:40.170
And then it also encourages
the model to keep trying

00:46:40.170 --> 00:46:41.880
and don't be lazy.

00:46:41.880 --> 00:46:46.710
So if this hypothesis
is true, then

00:46:46.710 --> 00:46:51.000
understand how these
different reasoning strategies

00:46:51.000 --> 00:46:54.280
work within the model
becomes even more important.

00:46:57.510 --> 00:47:01.240
So back to implicit
reasoning, before our work,

00:47:01.240 --> 00:47:03.360
there were a bunch of
work, a great work that

00:47:03.360 --> 00:47:09.060
shows that LLMs truly struggle
with implicit reasoning.

00:47:09.060 --> 00:47:11.100
Some people show
that it struggles

00:47:11.100 --> 00:47:14.100
with compositional
reasoning like this.

00:47:14.100 --> 00:47:17.080
There is a famous
compositionality gap.

00:47:17.080 --> 00:47:20.230
And then some other
people show that LLMs,

00:47:20.230 --> 00:47:24.240
even GPT-4 at the time,
struggled with simple things

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:26.710
like comparative
reasoning or comparison.

00:47:26.710 --> 00:47:32.190
Like, Trump is 78,
Biden is 82, then

00:47:32.190 --> 00:47:34.835
Trump is younger or
older than Biden.

00:47:37.710 --> 00:47:43.750
So that was the
previous conclusions,

00:47:43.750 --> 00:47:48.650
but we had different opinions.

00:47:48.650 --> 00:47:52.790
It seems to us,
this all contribute

00:47:52.790 --> 00:47:59.180
to this autoregressive LLMs
and never truly reason or plan,

00:47:59.180 --> 00:48:00.470
narrative.

00:48:00.470 --> 00:48:05.340
But we had some
different beliefs,

00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:09.440
we have more faith, more
confidence in language models

00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:10.470
and transformers.

00:48:10.470 --> 00:48:15.770
So we want to more
carefully study this problem

00:48:15.770 --> 00:48:18.560
and to see whether we can
get some new insights.

00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:21.840
So we started with this
research questions-- first,

00:48:21.840 --> 00:48:25.350
can transformers learn
to reason implicitly,

00:48:25.350 --> 00:48:27.800
or are there
fundamental limitations

00:48:27.800 --> 00:48:32.630
that prohibit robust
acquisition of this skill?

00:48:32.630 --> 00:48:34.910
And then what factors?

00:48:34.910 --> 00:48:37.430
Is that the scale of the
training data, the distribution

00:48:37.430 --> 00:48:39.800
of the training data, or
the model architecture

00:48:39.800 --> 00:48:44.250
that control the acquisition
of implicit reasoning?

00:48:44.250 --> 00:48:44.750
OK?

00:48:44.750 --> 00:48:49.090
So with these two questions,
let's discuss the setup.

00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:57.080
The model and optimization
are pretty standard.

00:48:57.080 --> 00:48:59.490
I don't want to
introduce questions here.

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:01.370
So it's just like
a standard decoder

00:49:01.370 --> 00:49:05.520
only transformer in
GPT-2 architecture.

00:49:05.520 --> 00:49:07.040
And we'll also show
that the results

00:49:07.040 --> 00:49:09.330
are pretty robust to
different model scales.

00:49:09.330 --> 00:49:12.110
So you can have a deeper
model, but the conclusions

00:49:12.110 --> 00:49:13.340
are the same.

00:49:13.340 --> 00:49:18.440
And then the optimization
is also pretty standard.

00:49:18.440 --> 00:49:23.510
Then for the data we'll be
using will be synthetic data,

00:49:23.510 --> 00:49:25.550
because we want to
carefully control

00:49:25.550 --> 00:49:28.730
all the factors in
this investigation

00:49:28.730 --> 00:49:33.460
so that we can separate out
the problem we want to study.

00:49:35.990 --> 00:49:37.650
Let's just consider composition.

00:49:37.650 --> 00:49:39.840
We study both composition
and comparison,

00:49:39.840 --> 00:49:42.650
but let's focus on
composition for now.

00:49:42.650 --> 00:49:45.490
The data will look like just
a random knowledge graph.

00:49:45.490 --> 00:49:49.790
It has epsilon entities
and R relations.

00:49:49.790 --> 00:49:52.720
We set R to 200.

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:55.150
So then it's a bunch
of nodes and then

00:49:55.150 --> 00:49:58.420
these different
relations like this.

00:49:58.420 --> 00:50:02.240
Then these are the atomic facts.

00:50:02.240 --> 00:50:06.640
Like, superstition
singer is Stevie.

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:09.190
And then we can use
these atomic facts

00:50:09.190 --> 00:50:13.750
to get some inferred facts
or these two-hop compositions

00:50:13.750 --> 00:50:16.610
following the composition rule.

00:50:16.610 --> 00:50:22.300
If the head entity, r1, bridge
entity, and then bridge entity,

00:50:22.300 --> 00:50:26.500
r2, tail entity, then we
have this composition effect,

00:50:26.500 --> 00:50:29.570
like head entity,
r1, r2, tail entity.

00:50:29.570 --> 00:50:38.740
So it's like Barack,
wife, born in, 1964.

00:50:38.740 --> 00:50:43.420
So this composition, and you can
think, the data for comparison

00:50:43.420 --> 00:50:46.030
is similar.

00:50:46.030 --> 00:50:46.580
OK?

00:50:46.580 --> 00:50:49.960
Then the important
setup here is that we

00:50:49.960 --> 00:50:55.900
want to study inductive
learning of deduction rules.

00:50:55.900 --> 00:51:00.250
A fancy phrase, but let me
just decompose this for you.

00:51:00.250 --> 00:51:02.110
First of all, we
want the model, which

00:51:02.110 --> 00:51:06.050
is the decoder-only transformer,
to learn inductively.

00:51:06.050 --> 00:51:08.450
So just give a bunch
of training examples.

00:51:08.450 --> 00:51:11.720
You want to learn these rules.

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:14.090
And particularly, these
are deduction rules.

00:51:14.090 --> 00:51:17.210
So this is a typical
deduction rule.

00:51:17.210 --> 00:51:22.420
You start from some premises and
then you can deduce new facts.

00:51:22.420 --> 00:51:27.190
So this is inductive
learning of deduction rules.

00:51:27.190 --> 00:51:34.030
Then there are two
generalization settings,

00:51:34.030 --> 00:51:37.150
what we call the
in-distribution setting and then

00:51:37.150 --> 00:51:40.000
the out-of-distribution
setting, or this setting

00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:43.710
can also be called
systematic generalization.

00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:46.170
So it goes like this.

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:50.370
For the atomic facts, like those
edges in the knowledge graph,

00:51:50.370 --> 00:51:52.200
we split it into two sets--

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:56.370
the ID set and the OOD set.

00:51:56.370 --> 00:52:00.550
Then we all go through the same
rule, the composition rule,

00:52:00.550 --> 00:52:04.590
you get the corresponding
inferred facts.

00:52:04.590 --> 00:52:09.640
Then for this set of inferred
facts from the ID atomic facts,

00:52:09.640 --> 00:52:13.404
we split it into two sets--

00:52:13.404 --> 00:52:16.830
the training set
and the ID test set.

00:52:16.830 --> 00:52:26.010
So for these inferred facts,
put in the ID test sets.

00:52:26.010 --> 00:52:30.600
Even though we have not
seen these exact inferred

00:52:30.600 --> 00:52:34.450
facts in our training data, so
it's an unseen inferred effect,

00:52:34.450 --> 00:52:38.100
but we have seen all
of its constitutes

00:52:38.100 --> 00:52:43.540
like those atomic facts, being
composed with other atomic facts

00:52:43.540 --> 00:52:47.140
in these ID training sets.

00:52:47.140 --> 00:52:50.980
I know it's a bit of a
mouthful, but just bear with me.

00:52:50.980 --> 00:52:55.050
So for any of these inferred
facts in the ID test

00:52:55.050 --> 00:53:00.720
set, like, Barack,
wife, born in,

00:53:00.720 --> 00:53:06.300
1964, maybe you have not
seen that exact inferred fact

00:53:06.300 --> 00:53:08.220
in your training
data, but you have

00:53:08.220 --> 00:53:12.000
seen, like for
example, the relation,

00:53:12.000 --> 00:53:14.770
like Barack, wife, Michelle.

00:53:14.770 --> 00:53:17.550
You have seen these
atomic facts being

00:53:17.550 --> 00:53:19.680
composed with other relations.

00:53:19.680 --> 00:53:23.560
Also, similarly, you have
seen the other atomic facts,

00:53:23.560 --> 00:53:27.120
like Michelle born in
1964, being composed

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:29.350
with other atomic facts.

00:53:29.350 --> 00:53:33.370
You just haven't happened to
see that exact inferred fact,

00:53:33.370 --> 00:53:39.680
like Barack, wife, born
in 1964, so that you still

00:53:39.680 --> 00:53:43.830
need some generalization
to capture them.

00:53:43.830 --> 00:53:47.600
For the ODD test
set, then you still

00:53:47.600 --> 00:53:49.610
have seen all of
the atomic facts

00:53:49.610 --> 00:53:51.320
because otherwise,
the model doesn't

00:53:51.320 --> 00:53:54.590
know that these facts exist.

00:53:54.590 --> 00:53:59.210
But you have never seen any
of those atomic facts being

00:53:59.210 --> 00:54:02.210
composed in a
compositional fashion,

00:54:02.210 --> 00:54:04.730
being composed with
other relations.

00:54:04.730 --> 00:54:08.370
So in that sense, it's a
stronger generalization setting,

00:54:08.370 --> 00:54:11.130
so that's why it's called the
systematic generalization.

00:54:11.130 --> 00:54:13.970
If the model can do
this, then essentially,

00:54:13.970 --> 00:54:17.330
it means that the model has
truly learned this deduction

00:54:17.330 --> 00:54:23.870
rule, so that you can just apply
it to any arbitrary new facts.

00:54:23.870 --> 00:54:27.680
So that's really the
goal for this learning

00:54:27.680 --> 00:54:31.070
of deduction rules.

00:54:31.070 --> 00:54:35.150
Now, with all this vanity
set up in minds, let's look

00:54:35.150 --> 00:54:38.930
at some fun parts, look at
some interesting takeaways

00:54:38.930 --> 00:54:41.720
from our investigation.

00:54:41.720 --> 00:54:44.900
The first surprising
conclusion we found

00:54:44.900 --> 00:54:50.510
is that transformers can
learn to reason implicitly,

00:54:50.510 --> 00:54:55.610
but only through a
process called grokking.

00:54:55.610 --> 00:54:57.770
What is grokking?

00:54:57.770 --> 00:55:02.390
So let's look at
this figure for now.

00:55:02.390 --> 00:55:04.770
This curve is the
training accuracy.

00:55:04.770 --> 00:55:09.710
You can see, it quickly
goes to 100%, that means,

00:55:09.710 --> 00:55:12.980
the training has
overfit at these points.

00:55:12.980 --> 00:55:17.750
But if you look at
the ID test curve,

00:55:17.750 --> 00:55:20.840
when the model
first overfits here,

00:55:20.840 --> 00:55:24.560
the test accuracy
was still very low.

00:55:24.560 --> 00:55:30.710
And if you keep training the
model way beyond the saturation

00:55:30.710 --> 00:55:34.460
or overfitting, the overfitting
happens around like 10,000

00:55:34.460 --> 00:55:35.160
steps.

00:55:35.160 --> 00:55:42.190
And if you train it like 20
times more optimization steps,

00:55:42.190 --> 00:55:46.630
to these points, this is
log scale, for example,

00:55:46.630 --> 00:55:47.500
keep in mind.

00:55:47.500 --> 00:55:56.630
Then all of a sudden, at least,
the ID generalization happens.

00:55:56.630 --> 00:55:59.590
So the model gets to
100% test accuracy

00:55:59.590 --> 00:56:03.460
on composition in the ID split.

00:56:03.460 --> 00:56:06.940
Similarly, for comparison,
the overfitting

00:56:06.940 --> 00:56:09.220
happens very early
on, but it takes

00:56:09.220 --> 00:56:13.810
about 20 more times of
training optimization steps

00:56:13.810 --> 00:56:15.770
for generalization to happen.

00:56:15.770 --> 00:56:17.990
And interestingly,
for comparison,

00:56:17.990 --> 00:56:21.440
all the test accuracy
also get to 100%.

00:56:21.440 --> 00:56:23.780
So that's a problem
we'll look into later.

00:56:27.130 --> 00:56:30.820
So that's quite interesting.

00:56:30.820 --> 00:56:32.920
This transformers,
they can learn

00:56:32.920 --> 00:56:38.050
to reason implicitly but
only through rocking.

00:56:38.050 --> 00:56:41.440
And it's one of the first
studies that establishes

00:56:41.440 --> 00:56:45.170
this connection between
rocking and implicit reasoning,

00:56:45.170 --> 00:56:47.350
I believe.

00:56:47.350 --> 00:56:52.420
And we will investigate why
that happens in a second.

00:56:52.420 --> 00:56:56.690
Another immediate takeaway
here is that, as we just said,

00:56:56.690 --> 00:57:01.600
this level of systematicity
of generalization

00:57:01.600 --> 00:57:04.210
varies by the reasoning type.

00:57:04.210 --> 00:57:08.140
For compositional
reasoning, it never

00:57:08.140 --> 00:57:10.840
managed to learn
to generalize OOD.

00:57:10.840 --> 00:57:16.030
Well, for comparative reasoning,
OOD generalization did happen.

00:57:16.030 --> 00:57:18.700
So we want to
understand why there

00:57:18.700 --> 00:57:22.090
is such a difference as well.

00:57:22.090 --> 00:57:26.600
Then another interesting
takeaway here is that,

00:57:26.600 --> 00:57:30.730
so before this study, there
were already some studies

00:57:30.730 --> 00:57:32.340
that looked at grokking.

00:57:32.340 --> 00:57:36.420
And then try to understand,
why grokking happens?

00:57:36.420 --> 00:57:40.290
And under what conditions
would grokking happen?

00:57:40.290 --> 00:57:43.410
Then one common belief
in the literature

00:57:43.410 --> 00:57:48.600
was that there is a
critical data size.

00:57:48.600 --> 00:57:52.690
Once your total amount of data
surpasses a certain threshold,

00:57:52.690 --> 00:57:56.220
then grokking happens,
otherwise, it won't happen.

00:57:56.220 --> 00:58:02.010
But in our study, we tried
to study this hypothesis

00:58:02.010 --> 00:58:03.130
very carefully.

00:58:03.130 --> 00:58:07.740
Then we find that actually, it's
not the data size but the data

00:58:07.740 --> 00:58:10.710
distribution that matters.

00:58:10.710 --> 00:58:17.950
So in these experiments,
on the right, we keep the--

00:58:17.950 --> 00:58:20.620
so there is two variables.

00:58:20.620 --> 00:58:22.530
There is the total
data size, which

00:58:22.530 --> 00:58:24.790
is controlled by the
number of entities,

00:58:24.790 --> 00:58:27.510
and it's proportional
to the total number

00:58:27.510 --> 00:58:28.600
of training examples.

00:58:28.600 --> 00:58:33.570
And then there is phi, which
is the ratio of the inferred

00:58:33.570 --> 00:58:36.400
versus atomic facts.

00:58:36.400 --> 00:58:39.270
So if this number
is larger, that

00:58:39.270 --> 00:58:42.366
means that you have
more input effects

00:58:42.366 --> 00:58:44.730
than you have atomic effects.

00:58:44.730 --> 00:58:48.090
So on the right, we
keep the ratio fixed

00:58:48.090 --> 00:58:52.360
and increase the number of
entities or the total data size.

00:58:52.360 --> 00:58:55.620
Then you will see, the
speed of generalization

00:58:55.620 --> 00:59:01.074
is roughly the same when you
increase the total data size.

00:59:03.870 --> 00:59:05.760
It's, more or less, the same.

00:59:05.760 --> 00:59:10.860
However, when you keep
the data size fixed,

00:59:10.860 --> 00:59:13.980
so it's the same
number of entities,

00:59:13.980 --> 00:59:20.610
you just change the ratio
phi, from 3.6 to 18.

00:59:20.610 --> 00:59:26.370
Then you see, the
speed of generalization

00:59:26.370 --> 00:59:30.770
strongly positively
correlates with this ratio.

00:59:30.770 --> 00:59:33.650
If you have a higher ratio,
then the generalization

00:59:33.650 --> 00:59:35.870
will happen faster.

00:59:35.870 --> 00:59:38.030
And at some point,
it will be as fast

00:59:38.030 --> 00:59:42.080
as the overfitting
of the training data.

00:59:42.080 --> 00:59:44.870
So this really shows that
it's the critical data

00:59:44.870 --> 00:59:48.125
distribution that matters, not
the sheer size of your training

00:59:48.125 --> 00:59:48.625
data.

00:59:51.590 --> 00:59:55.730
Well, with all those
interesting takeaways,

00:59:55.730 --> 00:59:59.450
our job is not done here
yet, because there are still

00:59:59.450 --> 01:00:03.450
some very important questions
we have not answered.

01:00:03.450 --> 01:00:05.930
So why does grokking happen?

01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:10.500
What exactly happens during
this long grokking process?

01:00:10.500 --> 01:00:13.530
What's going on
within the model?

01:00:13.530 --> 01:00:16.790
And why does the level
of systematicity,

01:00:16.790 --> 01:00:20.310
in generalization, vary
by the reasoning type?

01:00:20.310 --> 01:00:22.670
So all of these
questions require

01:00:22.670 --> 01:00:26.180
a deeper look inside the model.

01:00:26.180 --> 01:00:29.090
That's the mechanistic
interpretation part

01:00:29.090 --> 01:00:32.420
of this study.

01:00:32.420 --> 01:00:36.050
So we will use some
popular and nowadays,

01:00:36.050 --> 01:00:39.120
very standard mechanistic
interpretation tools.

01:00:39.120 --> 01:00:41.450
One is logit lens.

01:00:41.450 --> 01:00:45.860
So here, we apply
the internal states

01:00:45.860 --> 01:00:50.480
at some position
within the transformer

01:00:50.480 --> 01:00:54.920
and multiply it with the
output embedding matrix

01:00:54.920 --> 01:00:58.550
so that we can get a
distribution over the output

01:00:58.550 --> 01:00:59.310
vocabulary.

01:00:59.310 --> 01:01:04.040
So we get a peek into what
this internal representation

01:01:04.040 --> 01:01:06.000
is about.

01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:10.195
Then we will also use the
so-called causal tracing.

01:01:14.480 --> 01:01:18.110
In a nutshell, this
technique allows

01:01:18.110 --> 01:01:25.040
you to quantitatively
measure the amount

01:01:25.040 --> 01:01:32.140
of the impact of a certain
internal state to the output.

01:01:32.140 --> 01:01:35.600
So it ranges from 0 to 1.

01:01:35.600 --> 01:01:39.880
If it's closer to 1, that
means that this position,

01:01:39.880 --> 01:01:45.475
this internal state has a larger
impact on the final decision.

01:01:45.475 --> 01:01:48.370
I won't get into
the very details

01:01:48.370 --> 01:01:52.630
because that will
take too much time.

01:01:52.630 --> 01:01:56.360
So with this mechanistic
interpretation techniques,

01:01:56.360 --> 01:02:00.130
now, we can try to
find or discover,

01:02:00.130 --> 01:02:06.280
rather, that the corresponding
generalized circuits formed

01:02:06.280 --> 01:02:09.250
for a different reason types.

01:02:09.250 --> 01:02:13.180
And this is what we
found, very beautiful.

01:02:13.180 --> 01:02:17.320
We find that for
composition, after grokking,

01:02:17.320 --> 01:02:22.100
the transformer will learn a
generalizing circuit like this.

01:02:22.100 --> 01:02:25.340
It's a kind of
dangerous circuit.

01:02:25.340 --> 01:02:27.260
So this is the input
to the transformer.

01:02:27.260 --> 01:02:29.780
You have the head
entity, r1, and r2.

01:02:29.780 --> 01:02:32.350
And then this is
the layer 0, this

01:02:32.350 --> 01:02:35.380
is layer 8, the final layer.

01:02:35.380 --> 01:02:40.630
Then the first few
layers of the transformer

01:02:40.630 --> 01:02:43.540
will essentially do two things--

01:02:43.540 --> 01:02:48.190
for these parts, it will process
the first hop, essentially,

01:02:48.190 --> 01:02:53.410
to look at h and r1, and then
find the bridge entity, b.

01:02:53.410 --> 01:02:56.500
Here, we're using
logical density here.

01:02:56.500 --> 01:03:02.140
So it has memorized these atomic
facts in the first few layers

01:03:02.140 --> 01:03:08.380
to the extent that it can
reliably predict b at layer 5.

01:03:08.380 --> 01:03:12.910
Then another thing that
needs to be done here

01:03:12.910 --> 01:03:17.560
is that it needs to defer
the processing of r2.

01:03:17.560 --> 01:03:21.600
So you cannot forget about r2
because r2 needs to be used

01:03:21.600 --> 01:03:22.100
later.

01:03:22.100 --> 01:03:22.980
Even though it.

01:03:22.980 --> 01:03:26.300
doesn't need to use r2
in the early layers,

01:03:26.300 --> 01:03:31.170
it needs to use r2 when it has
found b, the bridge entity.

01:03:31.170 --> 01:03:31.670
OK?

01:03:31.670 --> 01:03:34.600
So once the bridge
entity is identified,

01:03:34.600 --> 01:03:39.410
then in the internal states,
then it can be combined with r2.

01:03:39.410 --> 01:03:46.660
And then it has memorized the
atomic facts that b, r2 is t.

01:03:46.660 --> 01:03:50.320
So then you can combine the
bridge entity and the data

01:03:50.320 --> 01:03:54.070
processing of r2 to predict t.

01:03:54.070 --> 01:03:57.950
So that's the generalizing
circuit for composition,

01:03:57.950 --> 01:04:00.530
it has two clear stages.

01:04:00.530 --> 01:04:03.760
And that will also determine
its generalization behavior

01:04:03.760 --> 01:04:06.520
that we'll analyze later.

01:04:06.520 --> 01:04:10.360
But on the other hand, the
comparison relationship

01:04:10.360 --> 01:04:13.000
or reasoning has a
different circuit, what

01:04:13.000 --> 01:04:15.700
we call a parallel circuit.

01:04:15.700 --> 01:04:19.270
Remember, for comparison, it's
like, you have two entities,

01:04:19.270 --> 01:04:20.450
e1 and e2.

01:04:20.450 --> 01:04:27.880
You have some attributes of this
entity, like, this is Trump,

01:04:27.880 --> 01:04:28.870
this is Biden.

01:04:28.870 --> 01:04:32.050
And then Trump is v1 is 78.

01:04:32.050 --> 01:04:34.120
Biden is 82.

01:04:34.120 --> 01:04:42.330
Then the prediction is like,
Trump is younger or older

01:04:42.330 --> 01:04:46.390
or equals than Biden, right?

01:04:46.390 --> 01:04:50.260
Then the circuit here is
more of a parallel circuit.

01:04:50.260 --> 01:04:53.010
So the first few layers
of the transformer

01:04:53.010 --> 01:04:58.530
will learn to, in parallel,
retrieve the attribute values,

01:04:58.530 --> 01:05:00.960
like 78 and 82.

01:05:00.960 --> 01:05:05.790
Then the upper layers will
use these retrieved values

01:05:05.790 --> 01:05:11.010
to do the comparison and then
to predict the final answer,

01:05:11.010 --> 01:05:15.470
whether it's smaller
than, equal, or larger.

01:05:15.470 --> 01:05:15.970
OK?

01:05:15.970 --> 01:05:20.080
So given the reason through
mechanical interpretation,

01:05:20.080 --> 01:05:24.240
we can find the generalizing
circuit's configuration

01:05:24.240 --> 01:05:28.600
of different reasoning types,
and they are, indeed, different.

01:05:28.600 --> 01:05:31.410
And this different
configuration will

01:05:31.410 --> 01:05:35.920
determine their level of
systematicity of generalization.

01:05:38.790 --> 01:05:43.260
But before that, let
me share a simple way

01:05:43.260 --> 01:05:47.670
to fix or to improve this
systematic generalization,

01:05:47.670 --> 01:05:52.356
especially for composition.

01:05:52.356 --> 01:05:55.050
So as we showed earlier,
for composition,

01:05:55.050 --> 01:05:58.470
OOD generalization
never happened.

01:05:58.470 --> 01:05:59.280
Why?

01:05:59.280 --> 01:06:02.800
If you look at this
circuit and think about it,

01:06:02.800 --> 01:06:07.300
that becomes obvious.

01:06:07.300 --> 01:06:12.390
So for the ODD
generalization to happen,

01:06:12.390 --> 01:06:14.770
the model needs to
do a few things.

01:06:14.770 --> 01:06:19.070
It needs to first
memorize a copy

01:06:19.070 --> 01:06:22.770
of the first half atomic
facts, like h, r1,

01:06:22.770 --> 01:06:26.670
b in the lower layers
of the transformer,

01:06:26.670 --> 01:06:30.170
so that you can find the
bridge entity at the layer 5.

01:06:30.170 --> 01:06:34.520
Then it also needs
to store a copy

01:06:34.520 --> 01:06:39.800
of the second half atomic
facts, but in the upper layers,

01:06:39.800 --> 01:06:44.420
because the second half has a
delayed processing, so it needs

01:06:44.420 --> 01:06:47.540
to somehow store
the atomic facts,

01:06:47.540 --> 01:06:54.230
b, r2, t here, not in the lower
layer, but in the upper layer.

01:06:54.230 --> 01:06:56.730
However, for the
OOD generalization,

01:06:56.730 --> 01:06:59.360
remember, our
definition of ODD is

01:06:59.360 --> 01:07:02.240
that none of these
atomic facts has

01:07:02.240 --> 01:07:07.310
been seen during training to
be composed with other facts.

01:07:07.310 --> 01:07:11.660
In that situation, the
model has no incentive

01:07:11.660 --> 01:07:17.000
to store this second half of the
atomic fact in the upper layers.

01:07:17.000 --> 01:07:21.180
It only seen this atomic
fact individually,

01:07:21.180 --> 01:07:25.500
so it can easily memorize
them in the lower layers,

01:07:25.500 --> 01:07:27.680
but it doesn't
have the incentive

01:07:27.680 --> 01:07:31.460
to store another copy
in the upper layers,

01:07:31.460 --> 01:07:34.640
because that requires
extra effort.

01:07:34.640 --> 01:07:39.650
That's why ODD generalization
of composition never happens.

01:07:39.650 --> 01:07:43.620
Well, for comparison, you don't
have this stage of processing.

01:07:43.620 --> 01:07:47.610
So you only need to store one
copy of the atomic effect.

01:07:47.610 --> 01:07:52.020
And you can retrieve the
value in the lower layers

01:07:52.020 --> 01:07:53.940
here for the comparison.

01:07:53.940 --> 01:07:55.970
So you don't have
that issue, and that's

01:07:55.970 --> 01:08:00.200
why ODD generalization of
comparison does happen.

01:08:00.200 --> 01:08:01.200
OK?

01:08:01.200 --> 01:08:05.330
And to further validate
this hypothesis,

01:08:05.330 --> 01:08:07.440
we did an intervention here.

01:08:07.440 --> 01:08:10.370
So if that is indeed
the case, that

01:08:10.370 --> 01:08:12.590
is because the model
doesn't have an incentive

01:08:12.590 --> 01:08:16.550
to store this atomic
fact in the upper layers,

01:08:16.550 --> 01:08:22.310
then we just need to do some
cross-layer parameter sharing.

01:08:22.310 --> 01:08:26.560
So you tie the weights
of the lower layers

01:08:26.560 --> 01:08:28.760
with the weights
of the upper layer,

01:08:28.760 --> 01:08:30.740
so it's a parameter sharing.

01:08:30.740 --> 01:08:34.670
And then if the lower layer
has memorized the atomic facts,

01:08:34.670 --> 01:08:37.840
then the upper layer will
get a copy of it as well.

01:08:37.840 --> 01:08:39.490
So we did that intervention.

01:08:39.490 --> 01:08:43.240
And it turns out, boom,
all the generalization

01:08:43.240 --> 01:08:46.700
starts to happen
for composition.

01:08:46.700 --> 01:08:49.220
So this further
validates the hypothesis.

01:08:49.220 --> 01:08:51.790
It's a bit dense, but I
think that this is probably

01:08:51.790 --> 01:08:58.210
one of the most interesting
slides or analysis of this work.

01:08:58.210 --> 01:09:07.840
So remember, we try to
understand what exactly

01:09:07.840 --> 01:09:10.510
is going on during grokking.

01:09:10.510 --> 01:09:13.600
We said that the
grokking process

01:09:13.600 --> 01:09:19.350
is when these generalizing
circuits start to form,

01:09:19.350 --> 01:09:21.479
but in what exact way?

01:09:21.479 --> 01:09:22.859
That's still unclear.

01:09:22.859 --> 01:09:28.490
So through causal
tracing, we can actually

01:09:28.490 --> 01:09:32.695
identify what exactly is
going on during grokking.

01:09:39.170 --> 01:09:42.744
Let's first focus
on this figure.

01:09:51.380 --> 01:09:52.859
Let me first say this.

01:09:52.859 --> 01:09:59.330
So we believe, grokking is the
phase transition from rooted

01:09:59.330 --> 01:10:01.880
learning to generalization.

01:10:01.880 --> 01:10:07.490
So grokking starts
when the model

01:10:07.490 --> 01:10:09.896
has overfit the training data.

01:10:09.896 --> 01:10:16.150
But at that point, the model
has only done rote learning,

01:10:16.150 --> 01:10:21.420
so it just [INAUDIBLE] memorized
all the training data, including

01:10:21.420 --> 01:10:25.660
the inferred facts, but not
actually in a generalizable way.

01:10:25.660 --> 01:10:28.240
But because it has
memorized all of them,

01:10:28.240 --> 01:10:31.200
so the training
loss will be zero,

01:10:31.200 --> 01:10:33.510
but it doesn't mean
that it has captured

01:10:33.510 --> 01:10:35.170
this generalizing circuit.

01:10:35.170 --> 01:10:38.790
Then the grokking
process is essentially

01:10:38.790 --> 01:10:43.020
the phase transition from
the initial rote learning

01:10:43.020 --> 01:10:45.310
to the generalization.

01:10:45.310 --> 01:10:49.110
And it's where this generalizing
circuit gets formed.

01:10:49.110 --> 01:10:55.290
And now, let's analyze how
exactly it gets formed.

01:10:55.290 --> 01:10:59.530
The first thing we will
look at is this figure.

01:10:59.530 --> 01:11:03.090
We look at two things.

01:11:03.090 --> 01:11:09.180
So S, 5, r1 is this position.

01:11:12.790 --> 01:11:14.770
This is S stands for states.

01:11:14.770 --> 01:11:19.720
So this is a state of layer
5 as the position of r1.

01:11:19.720 --> 01:11:24.150
So it's this one, S5, r1.

01:11:24.150 --> 01:11:28.710
Then we can use
logical dense to decode

01:11:28.710 --> 01:11:35.370
what's captured in that state
representation, in that state.

01:11:35.370 --> 01:11:39.390
You can see, the
grokking process,

01:11:39.390 --> 01:11:45.690
and similarly, we can do that
for S, r5, r2, so this position.

01:11:45.690 --> 01:11:51.480
So once grokking starts,
the model already has,

01:11:51.480 --> 01:11:55.600
and this is MRR, so it's
the Mean Reciprocal Rank.

01:11:55.600 --> 01:12:00.330
But basically, it
just tells you,

01:12:00.330 --> 01:12:05.970
the ranking of the bridge
NCP in the logic dense.

01:12:05.970 --> 01:12:10.010
If it's one, it means that
the state here will always

01:12:10.010 --> 01:12:13.670
predict the bridge entity
b, so that means that it

01:12:13.670 --> 01:12:15.300
has encoded this information.

01:12:15.300 --> 01:12:19.050
So when the grokking
starts, b is already there,

01:12:19.050 --> 01:12:20.970
the MRR equals 1.

01:12:20.970 --> 01:12:23.780
So that means, this
state here always

01:12:23.780 --> 01:12:27.800
predicts the bridge entity
b, which is what we want.

01:12:27.800 --> 01:12:31.920
We want the first half to
get us the bridge entity.

01:12:31.920 --> 01:12:33.240
That is great.

01:12:33.240 --> 01:12:36.410
But remember, we
also need the model

01:12:36.410 --> 01:12:41.510
to do a delayed processing
of r2, so that at this point,

01:12:41.510 --> 01:12:47.720
it can combine b and r2 to
predict the tail entity t.

01:12:47.720 --> 01:12:54.800
But if you look at MRR of
r2 at this position, when

01:12:54.800 --> 01:13:01.100
grokking starts, it
does not have r2 there.

01:13:01.100 --> 01:13:05.840
So it has to be here, but
it doesn't have r2 here.

01:13:05.840 --> 01:13:11.070
Despite that, so this
is on the training set.

01:13:11.070 --> 01:13:14.430
So the model has a
training loss of 0.

01:13:14.430 --> 01:13:19.460
So the model can perfectly
predict the tail entity,

01:13:19.460 --> 01:13:24.770
despite not using r2
here, to combine with b.

01:13:24.770 --> 01:13:29.220
That means the model is
just doing rote learning.

01:13:29.220 --> 01:13:34.190
So it just memorize that,
hey, whenever I see h, r1, r2,

01:13:34.190 --> 01:13:37.670
then I will predict t,
but it's not actually

01:13:37.670 --> 01:13:41.270
doing this staged thing like
getting the bridge entity

01:13:41.270 --> 01:13:45.320
and then combined with r2
to use another atomic fact

01:13:45.320 --> 01:13:47.630
and do the reasoning.

01:13:47.630 --> 01:13:51.560
But through the grokking
process, you can see,

01:13:51.560 --> 01:13:54.750
the r2 gradually increases.

01:13:54.750 --> 01:13:59.970
So at the end of a grokking,
it always predicts r2.

01:13:59.970 --> 01:14:02.670
Then you have t here,
you have r2 here,

01:14:02.670 --> 01:14:07.060
then you can actually
recall the atomic facts here

01:14:07.060 --> 01:14:10.330
to predict the t entity.

01:14:10.330 --> 01:14:17.080
So this is further corroborated
by the causal tracing results

01:14:17.080 --> 01:14:18.310
here.

01:14:18.310 --> 01:14:21.610
So remember, through
causal tracing,

01:14:21.610 --> 01:14:26.290
we can quantify the causal
strength of each state

01:14:26.290 --> 01:14:29.380
to the final prediction.

01:14:29.380 --> 01:14:33.310
And this is the causal
strength of each state

01:14:33.310 --> 01:14:34.970
at the beginning of grokking.

01:14:34.970 --> 01:14:37.220
And this is after grokking.

01:14:37.220 --> 01:14:41.470
Now, we can calculate the
difference between the two

01:14:41.470 --> 01:14:44.140
and find their diff.

01:14:44.140 --> 01:14:52.390
You can see, the diff mainly
happens here at S5, r1.

01:14:52.390 --> 01:14:55.670
So that means, in the
beginning of grokking,

01:14:55.670 --> 01:14:58.910
even though the model has
the bridge entity be here,

01:14:58.910 --> 01:15:00.710
it does not use it.

01:15:00.710 --> 01:15:05.530
It just did rote learning, so it
directly predicts the t entity.

01:15:05.530 --> 01:15:08.200
But the grokking
process is the process

01:15:08.200 --> 01:15:12.050
of the model learning to use
this bridge entity properly,

01:15:12.050 --> 01:15:14.860
so giving it a stronger
causal strength

01:15:14.860 --> 01:15:16.460
to the final prediction.

01:15:16.460 --> 01:15:21.520
And this, combined with
the fact that we just shown

01:15:21.520 --> 01:15:24.940
that through logic
dense, that's r2,

01:15:24.940 --> 01:15:29.350
the grokking process is where
these r2 start to emerge.

01:15:29.350 --> 01:15:34.630
These, combined,
validates the hypothesis

01:15:34.630 --> 01:15:37.990
that this grokking
process is really

01:15:37.990 --> 01:15:42.880
the process of forming these
generalized circuits, this stage

01:15:42.880 --> 01:15:46.600
circuits that we just
shown for composition.

01:15:46.600 --> 01:15:50.380
And we can actually explain,
even though we didn't

01:15:50.380 --> 01:15:53.350
do any proof of any
sort, but you can roughly

01:15:53.350 --> 01:15:55.810
understand why this kind
of grasping behavior

01:15:55.810 --> 01:15:58.150
happens through the
circuit efficiency

01:15:58.150 --> 01:16:00.460
and the regularization.

01:16:00.460 --> 01:16:04.290
The generalizing circuits
is much more efficient

01:16:04.290 --> 01:16:11.090
than the memorizing circuit,
the circuit for rote learning.

01:16:11.090 --> 01:16:14.440
And because you also
have regularization,

01:16:14.440 --> 01:16:17.400
the l2 regularization
term, for example.

01:16:17.400 --> 01:16:21.670
Then if you just keep training,
even after overfitting,

01:16:21.670 --> 01:16:24.880
the regularization
term will decrease.

01:16:24.880 --> 01:16:28.530
And then it will gradually
favor the circuit

01:16:28.530 --> 01:16:32.770
with higher efficiency, which
is the generalizing circuit.

01:16:32.770 --> 01:16:37.080
So that's why you find it still
beneficial to train way over

01:16:37.080 --> 01:16:38.880
beyond overfitting,
because that's

01:16:38.880 --> 01:16:40.980
when the regularization
starts to kick in

01:16:40.980 --> 01:16:45.240
and gets you the more
efficient circuit.

01:16:45.240 --> 01:16:48.850
But at least let me give you
the definition of planning,

01:16:48.850 --> 01:16:51.450
because that, in itself,
is a very confusing thing

01:16:51.450 --> 01:16:53.280
for various reasons.

01:16:53.280 --> 01:16:55.930
So in the context
of language agents,

01:16:55.930 --> 01:16:58.140
we will work with this
simplified definition

01:16:58.140 --> 01:16:58.930
of planning.

01:16:58.930 --> 01:17:02.590
Given a goal, g, decides
on the sequence of actions,

01:17:02.590 --> 01:17:06.330
a0 to an, that will
lead to a state that

01:17:06.330 --> 01:17:09.840
will pass the goal test, g.

01:17:09.840 --> 01:17:12.510
So of course, this is
all very simplified

01:17:12.510 --> 01:17:16.260
because we don't talk about the
state space, the observation

01:17:16.260 --> 01:17:16.990
space.

01:17:16.990 --> 01:17:20.920
And then the actions, they're
not just atomic actions,

01:17:20.920 --> 01:17:23.430
they have
preconditions that have

01:17:23.430 --> 01:17:25.930
to be met before they can be
taken, and so on and so forth.

01:17:25.930 --> 01:17:28.860
But for this
purpose, I think it's

01:17:28.860 --> 01:17:31.090
enough to have this definition.

01:17:31.090 --> 01:17:37.920
Then with this definition, we
can analyze these general trends

01:17:37.920 --> 01:17:40.600
in planning settings
for language agents,

01:17:40.600 --> 01:17:43.320
compared with the classic
planning setting, which

01:17:43.320 --> 01:17:46.800
has been studied for decades.

01:17:46.800 --> 01:17:50.310
Generally, I think,
the expressiveness

01:17:50.310 --> 01:17:54.250
in goal specification is
drastically increasing.

01:17:54.250 --> 01:17:58.770
So now, we can express our goal
in natural language as opposed

01:17:58.770 --> 01:18:01.700
to some formal language,
which is usually much more

01:18:01.700 --> 01:18:04.520
limited in expressiveness.

01:18:04.520 --> 01:18:07.460
And also, we have a
substantially expanded

01:18:07.460 --> 01:18:09.860
or open-ended action space.

01:18:09.860 --> 01:18:13.190
So instead of some very
constrained action space,

01:18:13.190 --> 01:18:15.690
like, hey, you have a
robot, you can move forward,

01:18:15.690 --> 01:18:17.550
you can turn left, and
so on and so forth.

01:18:17.550 --> 01:18:21.140
Now, you can have an even
open-ended action space.

01:18:21.140 --> 01:18:24.410
And we will see some
examples soon later.

01:18:24.410 --> 01:18:28.370
Then because of those,
it becomes increasingly

01:18:28.370 --> 01:18:32.270
hard to automate the goal test.

01:18:32.270 --> 01:18:34.730
Imagine, you have a
web agent that is just

01:18:34.730 --> 01:18:36.270
doing things on the web.

01:18:36.270 --> 01:18:41.210
Then a lot of the time, you just
simply cannot just write a goal

01:18:41.210 --> 01:18:42.850
test beforehand.

01:18:42.850 --> 01:18:46.400
What the goal
state will be like?

01:18:46.400 --> 01:18:48.740
But that's fine,
because fuzziness

01:18:48.740 --> 01:18:52.430
is really inherent
part of this world.

01:18:52.430 --> 01:18:54.170
I'll skip this one.

01:18:54.170 --> 01:19:00.110
So I just give some examples for
web agents, from our mighty web.

01:19:00.110 --> 01:19:05.310
You'll see, in terms of
the goal specification,

01:19:05.310 --> 01:19:12.140
the user can ask for anything
on an arbitrary website.

01:19:12.140 --> 01:19:15.900
So it's very broad
in natural language.

01:19:15.900 --> 01:19:19.050
And then in terms
of the action space,

01:19:19.050 --> 01:19:23.660
you have some broad
actions, like you can type,

01:19:23.660 --> 01:19:28.110
you can click, you can drag,
you can hover on some elements.

01:19:28.110 --> 01:19:33.270
But the actual actions, like
what elements you click on,

01:19:33.270 --> 01:19:39.440
these actual actions are
dynamically populated

01:19:39.440 --> 01:19:41.490
on each web page.

01:19:41.490 --> 01:19:43.110
So if you go to a
different web page,

01:19:43.110 --> 01:19:45.210
your action space
will be different.

01:19:45.210 --> 01:19:48.510
And it's a very
big action space.

01:19:48.510 --> 01:19:51.290
You have to discover on the fly.

01:19:51.290 --> 01:19:55.325
And of course, the goal
test is also very hard.

01:19:55.325 --> 01:19:58.510
And then there's another
example of trial planning

01:19:58.510 --> 01:20:00.470
that has some similar
characteristics,

01:20:00.470 --> 01:20:02.110
but we'll skip here.

01:20:02.110 --> 01:20:07.180
So I think these are some of
the general trends for language

01:20:07.180 --> 01:20:08.090
agent planning.

01:20:08.090 --> 01:20:11.150
And I think, these
are good trends.

01:20:11.150 --> 01:20:12.950
Yeah, it makes things
more challenging,

01:20:12.950 --> 01:20:16.150
but for the better,
because now, we

01:20:16.150 --> 01:20:21.730
can support much more realistic
and useful application scenarios

01:20:21.730 --> 01:20:23.560
with these language agents.

01:20:23.560 --> 01:20:29.320
So let's see what you all want
to talk about this, maybe,

01:20:29.320 --> 01:20:30.610
very quickly.

01:20:30.610 --> 01:20:34.750
But for people who are
interested in web agents

01:20:34.750 --> 01:20:36.850
or computer user
agents, I encourage

01:20:36.850 --> 01:20:39.070
you to look at these
series of work.

01:20:39.070 --> 01:20:42.640
Like Mind2Web, the first
LLM-based web agents, and then

01:20:42.640 --> 01:20:45.310
SeeAct, where we first
introduced visual perception

01:20:45.310 --> 01:20:48.190
into web agents,
and then UGround,

01:20:48.190 --> 01:20:51.640
where, for the first time, we
make a human-like embodiment

01:20:51.640 --> 01:20:53.330
for computer use agents.

01:20:53.330 --> 01:20:55.910
So you only perceive the
environment visually.

01:20:55.910 --> 01:20:58.610
There is no HTML or
anything like that.

01:20:58.610 --> 01:21:01.390
And then you directly do
pixel-level operations

01:21:01.390 --> 01:21:02.980
on the screen.

01:21:02.980 --> 01:21:06.760
And this minimalist
design actually

01:21:06.760 --> 01:21:08.990
works the best across the board.

01:21:08.990 --> 01:21:11.810
And then we will talk
about WebDreamer,

01:21:11.810 --> 01:21:17.660
which is model-based
planning for web agents.

01:21:17.660 --> 01:21:18.160
OK?

01:21:18.160 --> 01:21:21.280
So let's consider the
different planning paradigms

01:21:21.280 --> 01:21:23.560
for language agents.

01:21:23.560 --> 01:21:28.300
The most common one is
reactive planning or react.

01:21:28.300 --> 01:21:30.860
Imagine, each node
here is a web page.

01:21:30.860 --> 01:21:34.720
Then at each stage, you have
several candidate actions

01:21:34.720 --> 01:21:37.100
you can take that will
result in a different state.

01:21:37.100 --> 01:21:40.210
For reactive planning,
you just, at each stage,

01:21:40.210 --> 01:21:44.050
observe the environment, reason
about it, then make a decision,

01:21:44.050 --> 01:21:46.240
and commit to the
decision, and then

01:21:46.240 --> 01:21:49.910
that gets you to another state,
then you just keep doing this.

01:21:49.910 --> 01:21:52.850
So it's fast, it's
easy to implement,

01:21:52.850 --> 01:21:56.500
but the downside is that it's
greedy and it's short-sighted.

01:21:56.500 --> 01:21:59.520
So you often find yourself
stuck in some bad state.

01:21:59.520 --> 01:22:02.280
And there's no way out.

01:22:02.280 --> 01:22:04.690
Then naturally, when
we talk about planning,

01:22:04.690 --> 01:22:08.070
we just think about
search or tree search.

01:22:08.070 --> 01:22:11.920
So for tree search, compared
with reactive planning,

01:22:11.920 --> 01:22:14.260
you can do backtracking.

01:22:14.260 --> 01:22:20.490
You maintain a value assignment
for the states on your search

01:22:20.490 --> 01:22:21.750
frontier.

01:22:21.750 --> 01:22:25.080
Then you explore the
most promising branch.

01:22:25.080 --> 01:22:29.670
At some point, if you find that
not promising, you can backtrack

01:22:29.670 --> 01:22:34.060
and then you try to
explore another branch.

01:22:34.060 --> 01:22:37.830
So that gives you more
systematic exploration.

01:22:37.830 --> 01:22:41.310
But the downside
here is that it's

01:22:41.310 --> 01:22:44.430
in real-world environments
like the internet.

01:22:44.430 --> 01:22:47.100
There are a lot of
irreversible actions

01:22:47.100 --> 01:22:50.370
that makes backtracking
impossible or at least,

01:22:50.370 --> 01:22:51.130
very hard.

01:22:51.130 --> 01:22:53.640
And then a lot of
this exploration

01:22:53.640 --> 01:22:56.340
could also be unsafe or slow.

01:22:56.340 --> 01:23:01.380
To just show you how pervasive
this state-changing and

01:23:01.380 --> 01:23:04.690
irreversible actions are
in real-world environments,

01:23:04.690 --> 01:23:07.140
let's consider just amazon.com.

01:23:07.140 --> 01:23:11.340
On this single website,
you can have dozens, if not

01:23:11.340 --> 01:23:13.740
hundreds of these
state-changing actions.

01:23:13.740 --> 01:23:15.010
You can place an order.

01:23:15.010 --> 01:23:17.050
You can make a return.

01:23:17.050 --> 01:23:20.280
You can create an account that
you will agree to the terms

01:23:20.280 --> 01:23:21.040
and use.

01:23:21.040 --> 01:23:23.448
And then that has
legal implications.

01:23:23.448 --> 01:23:24.990
You can change your
privacy settings,

01:23:24.990 --> 01:23:27.074
and so on and so forth.

01:23:27.074 --> 01:23:32.280
If there is no like this
universal magical Undo button

01:23:32.280 --> 01:23:35.760
that you can just try a bunch
of things and then magically

01:23:35.760 --> 01:23:40.050
undo and then go back
to the initial state.

01:23:40.050 --> 01:23:43.860
So that makes tree search in
real-world environments hard.

01:23:43.860 --> 01:23:47.970
And then there's also the
safety and cost issues.

01:23:47.970 --> 01:23:51.860
So ideally, we want to
do model-based planning.

01:23:51.860 --> 01:23:56.765
Imagine, you have a world
model that at each stage,

01:23:56.765 --> 01:24:01.880
you can trigger the world
model to simulate the outcome

01:24:01.880 --> 01:24:04.730
of each candidate action.

01:24:04.730 --> 01:24:14.240
Then that gives you a chance
to evaluate the long-term value

01:24:14.240 --> 01:24:17.570
and the safety of
each candidate action

01:24:17.570 --> 01:24:21.060
before you have to commit to it.

01:24:21.060 --> 01:24:23.750
Then you find the most
promising candidate actions

01:24:23.750 --> 01:24:25.500
through simulation.

01:24:25.500 --> 01:24:28.520
Then you commit to it, take
it, assuming it's safe.

01:24:28.520 --> 01:24:30.210
And then you get
to another stage,

01:24:30.210 --> 01:24:33.210
then you can do
this all over again.

01:24:33.210 --> 01:24:36.300
So it's faster and safer
compared with tree search.

01:24:36.300 --> 01:24:40.100
And you can also still do
systematic exploration,

01:24:40.100 --> 01:24:45.980
but the downside is really how
to get this magical world model.

01:24:45.980 --> 01:24:48.260
Let's start with,
what is a world model?

01:24:48.260 --> 01:24:51.020
Because this is another
overloaded term.

01:24:51.020 --> 01:24:53.580
For our purpose here, we'll
take this definition--

01:24:53.580 --> 01:24:56.990
it's a computational model
of the environment transition

01:24:56.990 --> 01:24:58.340
dynamics.

01:24:58.340 --> 01:25:04.520
So basically, if I do this right
now, what would happen next?

01:25:04.520 --> 01:25:06.230
Very simple.

01:25:06.230 --> 01:25:07.730
Then if it's that
simple and it's

01:25:07.730 --> 01:25:12.170
so good, why hasn't it been
done for language agents yet?

01:25:12.170 --> 01:25:16.550
Well, the issue here
is, if you think

01:25:16.550 --> 01:25:20.780
about word models in the classic
deep learning literature, that's

01:25:20.780 --> 01:25:24.770
usually studied in
reinforcement learning, deep RL,

01:25:24.770 --> 01:25:28.100
where you have these simple
simulated environments that you

01:25:28.100 --> 01:25:31.950
can do millions of
times of trials,

01:25:31.950 --> 01:25:34.970
and then you can use that
to learn a world model

01:25:34.970 --> 01:25:37.280
for that simple environment.

01:25:37.280 --> 01:25:42.200
What we're dealing with here is
much more complicated, even just

01:25:42.200 --> 01:25:44.720
for a single website,
there could be hundreds

01:25:44.720 --> 01:25:45.890
of different web pages.

01:25:45.890 --> 01:25:47.540
On a single web
page, there could

01:25:47.540 --> 01:25:49.640
be hundreds of
different actions,

01:25:49.640 --> 01:25:53.410
and they can be constantly
changing, because the back end

01:25:53.410 --> 01:25:54.790
database is changing.

01:25:54.790 --> 01:25:57.770
And then this complexity
quickly compounds

01:25:57.770 --> 01:26:00.340
if we consider that there are
billions of other websites

01:26:00.340 --> 01:26:01.310
out there.

01:26:01.310 --> 01:26:06.460
In that sense, we need
a generalist world model

01:26:06.460 --> 01:26:08.380
for the internet.

01:26:08.380 --> 01:26:11.650
That seems very hard.

01:26:11.650 --> 01:26:16.000
Fortunately, we find that LLMs
can actually reasonably predict

01:26:16.000 --> 01:26:17.660
these state transitions.

01:26:17.660 --> 01:26:22.550
So in this example, if you ask
an LLM, if I click this icon,

01:26:22.550 --> 01:26:23.450
what will happen?

01:26:23.450 --> 01:26:26.390
Then it can recognize
that, hey, this is a shirt.

01:26:26.390 --> 01:26:28.160
And this is probably a product.

01:26:28.160 --> 01:26:32.000
So the next state will probably
be about some product details.

01:26:32.000 --> 01:26:33.610
And because it's
a shirt, so there

01:26:33.610 --> 01:26:37.040
will be sizing options
and other things.

01:26:37.040 --> 01:26:41.290
Because LLMs have this
common sense knowledge

01:26:41.290 --> 01:26:46.250
is trained on the internet so
it can do a reasonable job.

01:26:46.250 --> 01:26:49.150
Far from perfect,
but reasonable.

01:26:49.150 --> 01:26:52.850
So WebDreamer
leverages exactly that.

01:26:52.850 --> 01:26:56.590
We simulate a world model
for the internet using

01:26:56.590 --> 01:26:59.560
LLM, in this case, GPT-4o.

01:26:59.560 --> 01:27:04.280
Then when you're at a state, you
have a few candidate actions.

01:27:04.280 --> 01:27:07.270
You can use the world model
to simulate the outcome

01:27:07.270 --> 01:27:08.750
of each candidate action.

01:27:08.750 --> 01:27:10.850
You can even do
multistep simulation.

01:27:10.850 --> 01:27:14.930
You have another value function,
which is also simulated by LLM

01:27:14.930 --> 01:27:18.220
to tell you how much
progress you would

01:27:18.220 --> 01:27:20.810
have made going down that path.

01:27:20.810 --> 01:27:24.590
And then you can choose
the highest valued action.

01:27:24.590 --> 01:27:26.960
You take that action,
get to the next state,

01:27:26.960 --> 01:27:29.500
and then do this all over again.

01:27:29.500 --> 01:27:32.120
So that's model-based
planning for web agents.

01:27:32.120 --> 01:27:37.570
It's far from perfect, but it's
a reasonable starting point.

01:27:37.570 --> 01:27:41.750
In terms of results, we
evaluated on VisualWebArena use.

01:27:41.750 --> 01:27:45.810
So that model-based
planning is more accurate

01:27:45.810 --> 01:27:49.900
than reactive planning and
slightly trails tree search.

01:27:49.900 --> 01:27:52.230
But remember, tree
search is only possible

01:27:52.230 --> 01:27:55.440
because we are working in a
sandbox environment, which

01:27:55.440 --> 01:27:56.970
is VisualWebArena.

01:27:56.970 --> 01:28:00.360
If it's on the real websites,
then backtracking in tree search

01:28:00.360 --> 01:28:01.860
will become hard.

01:28:01.860 --> 01:28:04.320
And also, the
model-based planning

01:28:04.320 --> 01:28:07.220
is much cheaper and much faster.

01:28:07.220 --> 01:28:07.720
OK?

01:28:07.720 --> 01:28:10.120
So the takeaways for planning.

01:28:10.120 --> 01:28:13.080
I think, language agents are
expanding into this new planning

01:28:13.080 --> 01:28:17.610
scenarios, categorized by
the expressive but fuzzy goal

01:28:17.610 --> 01:28:21.510
specifications, the open-ended
action spaces, and the more

01:28:21.510 --> 01:28:24.240
difficult goal tests.

01:28:24.240 --> 01:28:28.770
But the language for reasoning
enables new planning abilities,

01:28:28.770 --> 01:28:31.870
like the word models,
model-based planning.

01:28:31.870 --> 01:28:36.060
And we didn't talk about today,
but the hierarchical planning

01:28:36.060 --> 01:28:39.930
and dynamic replanning
is also very important.

01:28:39.930 --> 01:28:43.060
And the best planning strategy
is dependent on the LLM.

01:28:43.060 --> 01:28:48.130
If you have a stronger base LLM,
it may require less scaffolding,

01:28:48.130 --> 01:28:52.500
so a planning strategy
could be more reactive.

01:28:52.500 --> 01:28:55.530
But generally, how to
improve planning in LLMs

01:28:55.530 --> 01:28:58.882
is still largely
an open question.

01:28:58.882 --> 01:29:03.030
I think, many people are
trying the recipe for o1 style

01:29:03.030 --> 01:29:07.150
reasoning, try to see whether
that works for planning,

01:29:07.150 --> 01:29:10.740
but that's still a
big open question.

01:29:10.740 --> 01:29:11.260
OK.

01:29:11.260 --> 01:29:13.560
So I think, we're
really just standing

01:29:13.560 --> 01:29:15.990
at the dawn of a long journey.

01:29:15.990 --> 01:29:18.360
We talked about planning,
reasoning, world models,

01:29:18.360 --> 01:29:19.620
and memory.

01:29:19.620 --> 01:29:22.390
But there are a lot of other
things we didn't talk about.

01:29:22.390 --> 01:29:24.690
And there's a lot to be done.

01:29:24.690 --> 01:29:28.950
Just a few immediate
future directions

01:29:28.950 --> 01:29:30.520
I find it interesting.

01:29:30.520 --> 01:29:32.970
I think, memory,
personalization,

01:29:32.970 --> 01:29:35.160
and continued
learning, we are really

01:29:35.160 --> 01:29:36.820
just scratching the surface.

01:29:36.820 --> 01:29:39.100
There is a whole lot to be done.

01:29:39.100 --> 01:29:41.310
How to enable agents
to continually learn

01:29:41.310 --> 01:29:44.390
from use and exploration?

01:29:44.390 --> 01:29:50.720
The reasoning, how to make
o1 or R1-style reasoning work

01:29:50.720 --> 01:29:52.610
for language agents,
where you need

01:29:52.610 --> 01:29:56.580
to deal with these fuzzy worlds
without reliable rewards?

01:29:56.580 --> 01:29:58.940
And how to integrate
these external actions

01:29:58.940 --> 01:30:00.480
and environmental states?

01:30:00.480 --> 01:30:02.460
And that's a big, open question.

01:30:02.460 --> 01:30:06.680
And I expect, there will be a
lot of study on this in 2025.

01:30:06.680 --> 01:30:09.560
Then planning, how to build
better world models instead

01:30:09.560 --> 01:30:11.220
of just simulator one?

01:30:11.220 --> 01:30:15.000
And how to balance the reactive
and model-based planning?

01:30:15.000 --> 01:30:17.270
Because you don't really
want to do simulation

01:30:17.270 --> 01:30:20.160
at every single step,
that's very costly.

01:30:20.160 --> 01:30:22.140
Even though humans
can do simulation,

01:30:22.140 --> 01:30:26.160
we don't do that for
every single decision,

01:30:26.160 --> 01:30:27.810
only for those difficult ones.

01:30:27.810 --> 01:30:31.400
But how to balance this reactive
and model-based planning

01:30:31.400 --> 01:30:34.400
is still an open question.

01:30:34.400 --> 01:30:36.260
How to sustain a long horizon?

01:30:36.260 --> 01:30:41.340
Then for safety, I think that's
a very pressing issue that

01:30:41.340 --> 01:30:43.790
really keeps me up at night.

01:30:43.790 --> 01:30:47.030
I think, the attack
surface of language agents

01:30:47.030 --> 01:30:49.170
is scarily broad.

01:30:49.170 --> 01:30:50.990
For web agents,
the attack surface

01:30:50.990 --> 01:30:53.780
is essentially the
entire internet.

01:30:53.780 --> 01:30:57.800
Someone can embed something
on a seemingly benign website.

01:30:57.800 --> 01:31:01.160
And for example, OpenAI
deep research agent

01:31:01.160 --> 01:31:04.140
can go there, visit there,
and get tricked by it,

01:31:04.140 --> 01:31:08.780
and then maybe reveal your
private information and things

01:31:08.780 --> 01:31:09.860
like that.

01:31:09.860 --> 01:31:13.440
So there are two general
types of safety issues.

01:31:13.440 --> 01:31:15.300
There's the endogenous risks.

01:31:15.300 --> 01:31:19.670
So these are the safety
risks originate from within,

01:31:19.670 --> 01:31:22.220
from the agent itself,
usually, because

01:31:22.220 --> 01:31:24.300
of the incompetency
of the agent.

01:31:24.300 --> 01:31:27.830
So it can mistakenly take
some irreversible actions

01:31:27.830 --> 01:31:28.950
that do harm.

01:31:28.950 --> 01:31:31.920
And then there are
exogenous risks.

01:31:31.920 --> 01:31:36.200
So these are the risks from
the external environment.

01:31:36.200 --> 01:31:36.900
OK?

01:31:36.900 --> 01:31:41.150
But there are also a lot
of exciting applications.

01:31:41.150 --> 01:31:46.780
Probably, the one with the
most clear business case

01:31:46.780 --> 01:31:49.580
is a genetic search
or deep research.

01:31:49.580 --> 01:31:52.390
If you have not used
[INAUDIBLE] Pro,

01:31:52.390 --> 01:31:55.480
or Google OpenAI deep
research, I highly

01:31:55.480 --> 01:31:57.020
encourage you to try that.

01:31:57.020 --> 01:32:02.320
And I think, something
big is being baked here.

01:32:02.320 --> 01:32:05.320
I think this will become
a huge thing in 2025.

01:32:05.320 --> 01:32:08.450
Then the, also,
workflow automation.

01:32:08.450 --> 01:32:11.620
And personally, I'm very
excited about developing agents

01:32:11.620 --> 01:32:13.600
for sciences.

01:32:13.600 --> 01:32:15.970
These are all very exciting.

01:32:15.970 --> 01:32:20.510
So for a more comprehensive
coverage on language agents,

01:32:20.510 --> 01:32:22.720
I encourage you to
check out our tutorial

01:32:22.720 --> 01:32:24.550
with Diyi, Shunyu, and Tao.

01:32:24.550 --> 01:32:27.020
We just did at EMNLP
two months ago.

01:32:27.020 --> 01:32:30.700
And all the materials,
like slides and videos,

01:32:30.700 --> 01:32:34.420
are available on our website.

01:32:34.420 --> 01:32:39.660
I thank all of my sponsors, and
be happy to take any questions.

